{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-8c77046861c1fd2c", "type": "tool_result", "content": "1\t \n2\t\n3\t Uncertainty-Aware Crossmodal Fusion for Classification of Animal Behavior \n4\t\n5\t Report GitHub Issue \n6\t × \n7\t\n8\t Title: \n9\t\n10\t Content selection saved. Describe the issue below: \n11\t\n12\t Description: \n13\t\n14\t Submit without GitHub \n15\t Submit in GitHub \n16\t\n17\t arXiv is now an independent nonprofit! \n18\t Learn more \n19\t &times; \n20\t\n21\t Back to arXiv \n22\t\n23\t Why HTML? \n24\t\n25\t Report Issue \n26\t\n27\t Back to Abstract \n28\t\n29\t Download PDF \n30\t\n31\t Abstract \n32\t 1 Introduction \n33\t\n34\t 2 Related Work \n35\t\n36\t 2.1 Acoustic Representations in Animal Bioacoustics \n37\t 2.2 Spectrogram-based Deep Learning Models \n38\t 2.3 Raw Waveform and Hybrid Audio Models \n39\t 2.4 Multimodal Fusion Strategies \n40\t 2.5 Uncertainty and Reliability Modeling in Multimodal Learning \n41\t\n42\t 3 Methods \n43\t\n44\t 3.1 Problem Definition \n45\t 3.2 Architecture Overview \n46\t\n47\t 3.3 Dual Audio Encoder \n48\t\n49\t Waveform encoder \n50\t Spectrogram encoder \n51\t\n52\t 3.4 Temporal Context Aggregation \n53\t 3.5 Gaussian Uncertainty Parameterization \n54\t 3.6 Dimension-wise Uncertainty Fusion \n55\t\n56\t 3.7 Training Objective \n57\t\n58\t Classification loss \n59\t Difficulty-Confidence loss \n60\t Variance regularizer \n61\t\n62\t 4 Experimental Setup \n63\t\n64\t 4.1 Datasets and Data Splits \n65\t\n66\t SoundWel Dataset \n67\t DogBark Dataset \n68\t Data Splits \n69\t\n70\t 4.2 Preprocessing and Data Augmentation \n71\t\n72\t Audio Preprocessing \n73\t Temporal Segmentation \n74\t Data Augmentation \n75\t\n76\t 4.3 Training Procedure \n77\t\n78\t Stage 1: Hyperparameter Optimization \n79\t Stage 2: Final Model Training \n80\t\n81\t 4.4 Test-Time Inference and Segment Aggregation \n82\t 4.5 Implementation Details \n83\t\n84\t 5 Results and Discussion \n85\t\n86\t 5.1 Quantitative Performance Comparison \n87\t\n88\t Role of temporal architecture \n89\t\n90\t 5.2 Per-Class Classification Analysis \n91\t 5.3 Prediction Calibration \n92\t 5.4 Prediction Confidence by Behavioral Context \n93\t 5.5 Fusion Weight Adaptation Under Targeted Modality Corruption \n94\t\n95\t 6 Conclusion \n96\t References \n97\t A Complete SoundWel Confusion Matrix \n98\t B Random Stratified Split Results \n99\t\n100\t License: arXiv.org perpetual non-exclusive license\n101\t\n102\tarXiv:2608.02104v1 [cs.SD] 03 Aug 2026 \n103\t\n104\t Uncertainty-Aware Crossmodal Fusion for Classification of Animal Behavior \n105\t\n106\t Ehsan Yaghoubi\n107\t\n108\t ehsan.yaghoubi@hswt.de \n109\t\n110\t Florian Haselbeck\n111\t\n112\t florian.haselbeck@hswt.de \n113\t\n114\t Abstract \n115\t Artificial intelligence offers substantial potential for acoustic monitoring of animals, from welfare assessment in precision livestock farming to wildlife conservation and ecological research, where vocalizations can indicate health, stress, and social states earlier and at lower cost than manual observation. However, recordings in these settings are obtained under uncontrolled conditions, including environmental noise, reverberation, overlapping calls, and sensors that degrade without notice. As a consequence, automated classification of animal vocalizations remains challenging, and the two dominant acoustic representations show complementary limitations: raw waveforms preserve temporal microstructure but degrade under clipping and reverberation, while log-Mel spectrograms capture harmonic organization but lose phase information and are sensitive to broadband noise. To address these challenges, we propose Uncertainty-Aware Fusion (UAF), a dual-stream framework that estimates Gaussian uncertainty for each representation and fuses them via uncertainty weighting. This mechanism assigns greater weight to the more confident representation with no reliability labels required. In a cross-species, identity-based evaluation excluding all individuals seen during training, UAF (mean pooling) achieves 59.4% accuracy / 39.7% macro F1 on the 17-class SoundWel pig vocalization benchmark and 73.1% accuracy / 71.5% macro F1 on the 3-class DogBark dataset, outperforming static-concatenation fusion by 15.7% and 20.4% relative macro F1, respectively. Ablations over four temporal aggregation strategies show that uncertainty fusion, rather than the temporal characteristics of animal calls, is the primary driver of the performance gain. \n116\t\n117\t keywords: \n118\tUncertainty Analysis , Multimodal Fusion , Bioacoustics , Animal Welfare , Precision Livestock Farming.\n119\t\n120\t † † journal: Computers and Electronics in Agriculture \n121\t\n122\t affiliation \n123\t [label1]organization=Weihenstephan-Triesdorf University of Applied Sciences,addressline=Department of Sustainable Agricultural and Energy Systems,\n124\tcity=Freising,\n125\tstreet=Am Staudengarten 1,,\n126\tpostcode=85354,\n127\tcountry=Germany\n128\t\n129\t 1 Introduction \n130\t\n131\t Precision livestock farming employs sensor-based monitoring to assess health, stress, and welfare, often at the level of the individual animal (Berckmans, 2017 ) . Acoustic sensing is a widely used modality, since it requires no animal contact, covers a pen from a fixed position, and adds comparatively little hardware per monitored unit (Coutant et al. , 2024 ; Shorten and Welten, 2021 ; Shorten and Hunter, 2023 ) . Vocalizations have furthermore been associated with pain, thermal discomfort, hunger, and social conflict (Manteuffel et al. , 2004 ) . In recent years, deep learning-based approaches trained on raw audio or spectrogram input have reached high accuracy on curated datasets, in welfare assessment (Coutant et al. , 2024 ) as well as in wildlife conservation (Kershenbaum et al. , 2025 ) and ecological research (Penar et al. , 2020 ) . This performance is largely obtained under favorable acquisition conditions and is often not reproduced in deployment, where signal quality is a limiting factor: recordings contain machinery and environmental noise, reverberation from hard surfaces, and overlapping calls from multiple animals, and sensors degrade gradually without this being detected at the time of recording. Since such predictions inform subsequent management decisions, quantifying the reliability of a prediction is of interest in addition to classification performance. \n132\t\n133\t Generally, there are two dominant acoustic representations, namely raw time-domain waveforms and log-Mel frequency spectrograms. While processing waveform signals preserves temporal microstructure and phase, it is sensitive to clipping and reverberation (Baevski et al. , 2020 ) . On the other hand, spectrogram-based models capture harmonic and frequency organization but discard phase and degrade under broadband noise (Stowell, 2022 ) . Therefore, neither representation is uniformly superior because the relative reliability shifts with recording conditions, which is a practically unavoidable reality in uncontrolled environments and field deployments. Existing dual-representation approaches (Kong et al. , 2020 ; Liang et al. , 2022 ) demonstrate that combining waveform and spectrogram features consistently outperforms either stream alone. However, these methods usually employ static fusion : both representations receive fixed weights regardless of their reliability for a given input. Under the variable acoustic conditions of real-world animal monitoring, static weights propagate noise from the degraded representation rather than suppressing it. Although uncertainty-aware fusion has been explored in audio-visual human emotion recognition (Tellamekala et al. , 2023 ; Xie et al. , 2024b ) , no prior work has adapted reliability-driven weighting to audio dual-representation fusion for bioacoustic classification. This is challenging because, unlike audio-visual fusion, both modalities originate from the same acoustic signal, requiring the model to determine which representation should be trusted in each step in time. \n134\t\n135\t We address this gap with Uncertainty-Aware Fusion (UAF) , a framework that dynamically weights waveform and spectrogram contributions using per-utterance latent uncertainty estimates (Fig.  1 ). First, modality-specific ResNet-18 encoders map each input to a temporal feature sequence, and a temporal aggregation module produces a single utterance-level embedding per modality. An uncertainty head then parameterizes a per-utterance diagonal Gaussian 𝒩 ​ ( 𝝁 m , 𝝈 m 2 ) mathcal{N}(boldsymbol{mu}_{m},boldsymbol{sigma}_{m}^{2}) for each modality m ∈ { A , F } min{A,F} , and dimension-wise uncertainty weights fuse the two means: each latent dimension is dominated by whichever modality is more confident for that input. Training optimizes a multi-term objective that learns from three streams, i.e., waveform, spectrogram, and a fusion branch. We evaluate on two publicly available bioacoustic benchmark datasets, i.e., SoundWel, a 17-class pig welfare vocalization (Briefer et al. , 2022 ) and DogBark, a dataset for context-dependent dog bark classification (Yin and McCowan, 2004 ) .\n136\tIn summary, our contributions are threefold: \n137\t\n138\t 1. \n139\t\n140\t We propose a dual-representation architecture for bioacoustic classification that processes raw waveform and log-Mel spectrogram inputs in parallel, capturing temporal and frequency cues that neither representation provides on its own. \n141\t\n142\t 2. \n143\t\n144\t We employ an uncertainty-aware fusion mechanism that estimates the reliability of each representation at every time step and weights their contributions accordingly. This directly targets a practical problem in real-world and barn recordings, where noise, clipping, or occlusion can degrade one representation while leaving the other intact. \n145\t\n146\t 3. \n147\t\n148\t We evaluate our framework on two bioacoustic benchmarks, SoundWel (pig, 17 classes) and DogBark (dog, 3 classes), and report ablation studies that show the gain from uncertainty-aware fusion relative to static concatenation baselines. \n149\t\n150\t 2 Related Work \n151\t\n152\t 2.1 Acoustic Representations in Animal Bioacoustics \n153\t\n154\t The time-domain waveform and the frequency spectrogram are two different representations of acoustic signals that can be used for behavioral classification of animals. Early approaches relied on hand-crafted features for specific species and monitoring objectives (Coutant et al. , 2024 ; Mcloughlin et al. , 2019 ) . These features are grouped into three types: time-domain descriptors such as call duration and inter-call intervals; frequency-domain descriptors including fundamental frequency, formants, and spectral centroid; and energy-related measures such as amplitude and signal-to-noise ratio. Dynamic features such as jitter and shimmer capture temporal micro-variations, while derived representations such as Mel-Frequency Cepstral Coefficients (MFCCs) and their temporal derivatives have become common in automatic classification systems (Li et al. , 2024 ) . For instance, pig distress calls exhibit higher pitch, longer duration, and noisier spectra, whereas chicken alarm calls are typically short, high-pitched bursts (Coutant et al. , 2024 ) . While hand-crafted features have enabled considerable progress in livestock monitoring (Mcloughlin et al. , 2019 ) and wildlife conservation, they require substantial domain expertise, may not generalize well across recording conditions or species, and can miss subtle, nonlinear interactions between temporal and spectral information. These limitations have motivated a shift toward learned representations via deep neural networks; however, whether time-domain waveforms, frequency-domain spectrograms, or both yield the best learned features remains an open question with distinct trade-offs in information preservation, computational cost, and noise robustness. \n155\t\n156\t 2.2 Spectrogram-based Deep Learning Models \n157\t\n158\t Spectrogram-based approaches have emerged as the dominant paradigm in bioacoustic deep learning, converting raw audio signals into time-frequency representations such as mel-spectrograms, MFCCs, or delta-mel features before applying, e.g., convolutional neural networks (CNNs) (Stowell, 2022 ; Xie et al. , 2024a ) . By treating spectrograms as images, computer vision approaches can learn hierarchical spatial patterns that capture both temporal and spectral dynamics, consistently outperforming traditional classifiers based on hand-crafted features in comparative studies (Ghani et al. , 2023 ; Bergler et al. , 2022 ) . Hybrid architectures that incorporate recurrent units, e.g., CNN–LSTM or CNN–GRU, further enhance performance by modeling temporal dependencies across spectrogram frames (Gupta et al. , 2021 ) . More recently, transformer-based models that treat the log-mel spectrogram as a sequence of 2-D patches, such as the Audio Spectrogram Transformer (AST) (Gong et al. , 2021 ) , achieve state-of-the-art results on large-scale audio benchmarks. The AVES (Animal Vocalization Encoder based on Self-Supervision) model (Hagiwara, 2023 ) extends this paradigm to animal vocalizations via self-supervised transformer pre-training, yielding strong transfer performance on bioacoustic classification. \n159\t\n160\t These spectrogram-based models have been successfully applied across diverse bioacoustic domains. In livestock applications, automated classification systems using multiple CNN backbones (e.g., AlexNet, ResNet, EfficientNet) have demonstrated high accuracy for dog vocalizations (Gómez-Armenta et al. , 2024 ) , while emotion recognition systems show promise for farm animal welfare monitoring (Briefer et al. , 2022 ) . For wildlife monitoring, large-scale species classification of birds and insects has leveraged CNNs to capture discriminative frequency patterns across taxa (Nanni et al. , 2020 ; Stowell, 2022 ) , while in marine bioacoustics, deep learning models have been developed for detecting baleen whale vocalizations (Schall et al. , 2024 ) . Recent frameworks such as ANIMAL-SPOT (Bergler et al. , 2022 ) provide end-to-end pipelines for detection and classification directly from spectrograms, showing strong generalization across multiple species and call types. \n161\t\n162\t Despite these empirical successes, spectrogram-based methods have known limitations. The time-frequency transformation inherently discards phase information, which may contain discriminative temporal microstructures relevant for certain call types. Model performance is sensitive to spectrogram resolution and preprocessing choices such as window size, hop length, and frequency binning, requiring careful tuning for each application. Furthermore, performance often degrades when deployed in real-world scenarios that differ from training conditions, particularly under varying noise profiles or recording equipment (Stowell et al. , 2019 ; Shorten and Hunter, 2023 ) . These robustness challenges suggest that relying solely on frequency-domain representations could be insufficient for generalizable bioacoustic monitoring systems. \n163\t\n164\t 2.3 Raw Waveform and Hybrid Audio Models \n165\t\n166\t Motivated by the potential information loss in spectrogram conversion, raw waveform modeling has gained attention as a complementary representation strategy. Raw waveform approaches often apply convolutional filters directly to time-domain audio signals, allowing the network to learn discriminative temporal features without explicit frequency transformation (Tokozume and Harada, 2017 ) . Self-supervised pre-training on raw waveforms—exemplified by wav2vec 2.0 (Baevski et al. , 2020 ) and HuBERT (Hsu et al. , 2021 ) —has demonstrated that contextual representations learned from large unlabeled audio corpora transfer effectively to downstream classification tasks. Large-scale pretrained models such as PANNs (Pretrained Audio Neural Networks) further show that fusing waveform- and spectrogram-level features improves audio pattern recognition. The Wavegram-Logmel-CNN variant in PANNs concatenates features from both domains and achieves better performance than either branch alone (Kong et al. , 2020 ) . The theoretical advantage of waveform models lies in preserving complete signal information, including phase relationships and fine-grained temporal structures that may be smoothed or lost during spectrogram computation. \n167\t\n168\t In bioacoustics, however, raw waveform modeling remains relatively underexplored compared to spectrogram-based approaches. Bravo Sanchez et al. demonstrate that SincNet-based waveform architectures can classify avian calls without spectrograms, establishing the viability of end-to-end waveform learning for bioacoustic tasks (Bravo Sanchez et al. , 2021 ) . Nevertheless, bioacoustic applications continue to face unique challenges, including data scarcity, high intra-species variability, and diverse recording conditions. More critically, although both representations offer complementary information, existing multi-representation approaches such as PANNs (Kong et al. , 2020 ) as well as the dual-stream waveform and log-mel fusion of Liang et al. ( 2022 ) rely on static, fixed-weight concatenation that does not adapt when one representation becomes less reliable under specific conditions. The key unresolved challenge is not whether to combine both representations but how to dynamically assess and respond to their relative reliability. \n169\t\n170\t 2.4 Multimodal Fusion Strategies \n171\t\n172\t The recognition that single representations may be insufficient has led to growing interest in multimodal integration, both across different sensor types and representations of the same audio signal. Baltrušaitis et al. provide the foundational taxonomy of multimodal learning strategies, distinguishing early fusion, late fusion, and intermediate fusion, and highlighting representation learning and fusion as the two central challenges (Baltrušaitis et al. , 2019 ) . In livestock monitoring, recent systems have combined acoustic signals with accelerometry and inertial sensors to improve the classification of behavioral states and welfare indicators (Vu et al. , 2024 ; Ferrero et al. , 2025 ) . These multimodal systems leverage complementary information from different sensing modalities to provide more robust predictions in noisy farm environments, where visual occlusion, acoustic interference, or sensor failures can degrade individual channels. \n173\t\n174\t However, most existing fusion strategies employ simple integration methods that assume equal reliability across modalities. Early fusion approaches concatenate raw features or low-level representations before classification, while late fusion combines independent predictions from modality-specific classifiers through averaging or voting. Intermediate strategies extract separate embeddings from each modality and concatenate them before the final classification layers (Baltrušaitis et al. , 2019 ) . These methods treat all modalities equally informative at all times, assigning fixed or learned but static weights that do not adapt to temporal variations in signal quality. Peng et al. show empirically that joint multimodal training suffers from a modality-imbalance problem and propose on-the-fly gradient modulation as a solution (Peng et al. , 2022 ) . \n175\t\n176\t This assumption of uniform reliability is problematic in real-world deployments, where waveform and spectrogram representations show input-dependent failure modes. Existing fusion methods amplify the noise from a degraded representation rather than suppress it, motivating reliability-adaptive weighting. \n177\t\n178\t 2.5 Uncertainty and Reliability Modeling in Multimodal Learning \n179\t\n180\t The concept of uncertainty-aware fusion, where modality contributions are dynamically weighted based on estimated reliability, has roots in Bayesian deep learning. Gal and Ghahramani showed that Monte Carlo dropout provides a computationally tractable approximation to Bayesian inference, yielding epistemic uncertainty estimates at test time without modifying the training procedure (Gal and Ghahramani, 2016 ) , while Kendall and Gal formalized the canonical decomposition into aleatoric uncertainty (irreducible data noise) and epistemic uncertainty (model uncertainty reducible with more data) (Kendall and Gal, 2017 ) . In the multimodal domain, Subedar et al. ( 2019 ) applied deep Bayesian variational inference to audio-visual activity recognition and demonstrated that per-modality uncertainty estimates improve robustness when one sensor stream is corrupted. Han et al. ( 2023 ) extended uncertainty-driven fusion to arbitrary numbers of views via Dempster-Shafer evidence fusion, enabling reliable classification under missing or degraded inputs. In multimodal emotion recognition, the COLD Fusion framework (Tellamekala et al. , 2023 ) introduced a principled approach by learning latent distributions over unimodal temporal contexts and constraining their variance to represent how informative each modality is toward the prediction task. By imposing calibration and ordinal ranking constraints on modality-wise uncertainty estimates, COLD Fusion enables adaptive weighting that favors more reliable modalities at each time step. \n181\t\n182\t 3 Methods \n183\t\n184\t The core idea of the proposed Uncertainty-Aware Fusion (UAF) framework is to replace static fusion weights for time-domain waveform and spectrogram representations with adaptive weights estimated directly from the learned features. This enables the model to quantify modality-specific uncertainty and suppress less reliable representations without requiring explicit reliability annotations. We first formulate the classification task and establish the notation used throughout the paper. We then describe the proposed framework in detail, including the overall architecture, unimodal waveform and spectrogram encoders, temporal aggregation, uncertainty estimation, the adaptive fusion strategy, and the corresponding training objective. \n185\t\n186\t 3.1 Problem Definition \n187\t\n188\t The task we address in this paper is to classify animal vocalizations into K K discrete behavioral categories. Given an audio recording, two complementary representations are extracted: a raw waveform x A ∈ ℝ L x_{A}inmathbb{R}^{L} and a three-channel stacked spectrogram x F ∈ ℝ C × H × W x_{F}inmathbb{R}^{Ctimes Htimes W} , where L L is the waveform length, C = 3 C=3 channels encode log-Mel, Δ Delta -Mel, and Δ ​ Δ DeltaDelta -Mel coefficients, and H × W Htimes W is the spectrogram spatial resolution (Young et al. , 2002 ; Takahashi et al. , 2016 ) . We consider three-channel spectrogram input rather than the one-channel log-Mel because the derivative channels capture short-term spectral dynamics (onset, offset, and formant-transition cues) that the log-Mel spectrogram discards (Young et al. , 2002 ) . The goal is to learn a mapping f : ( x A , x F ) ↦ y ^ ∈ { 1 , … , K } f:(x_{A},x_{F})mapstohat{y}in{1,ldots,K} that minimizes classification error while producing calibrated confidence estimates. \n189\t\n190\t 3.2 Architecture Overview \n191\t\n192\t Figure  1 illustrates the proposed Uncertainty-Aware Fusion (UAF) framework, which we realize in four steps. First, we perform modality encoding by passing each modality through a dedicated ResNet-18 encoder, producing a temporal sequence of T T feature vectors of dimension C e = 512 C_{e}=512 . These features are compressed by a temporal aggregation module into a single utterance-level embedding 𝐡 m ∈ ℝ C e mathbf{h}_{m}inmathbb{R}^{C_{e}} (see Sections 3.3 and 3.4 ). Then, we perform uncertainty parameterization by mapping each embedding to the mean and variance of a diagonal Gaussian, q m ​ ( z ∣ 𝐡 m ) = 𝒩 ​ ( 𝝁 m , diag ​ ( 𝝈 m 2 ) ) q_{m}(zmidmathbf{h}_{m})=mathcal{N}(boldsymbol{mu}_{m},mathrm{diag}(boldsymbol{sigma}_{m}^{2})) for m ∈ A , F min{A,F} , where A A and F F denote the waveform and spectrogram modalities. We use the predicted variance as an implicit measure of modality reliability, enabling the model to adaptively weight each modality without requiring explicit reliability annotations (see Section 3.5 ). Next, in uncertainty-weighted fusion step, we combine these Gaussians dimension-wise, giving more weight to whichever modality has lower variance in each latent dimension, and obtain a fused latent sample 𝐳 ∈ ℝ D z mathbf{z}inmathbb{R}^{D_{z}} (see Section 3.6 ). Finally, we do a joint classification by applying a linear classifier to fused representation 𝐳 mathbf{z} to obtain the final prediction, while two auxiliary classifiers are applied to 𝝁 A boldsymbol{mu}_{A} and 𝝁 F boldsymbol{mu}_{F} to provide modality-specific supervision. We train the whole model with a loss function inspired by (Tellamekala et al. , 2023 ) and (Xie et al. , 2024b ) that couples the predicted variances with the observed classification difficulty (see Section 3.7 ). \n193\t\n194\t Figure 1: Overview of the proposed Uncertainty-Aware Fusion (UAF) framework for animal vocalization classification. Audio waveform and frequency specgrogram are encoded separately and temporally aggregated. The resulting embeddings are mapped to Gaussian means and variances, and the uncertainty-aware fusion module combines the two modalities into a single fused distribution by assigning higher weights to the more certain modality. A vector sampled from this fused distribution, together with two auxiliary unimodal predictions, is used for the final classification. A difficulty-confidence loss couples each modality’s uncertainty to how hard its predictions are, while a regularization term keeps the uncertainties feasible for an end-to-end train. \n195\t\n196\t 3.3 Dual Audio Encoder \n197\t\n198\t We use separate encoders for the raw waveform and the spectrogram: \n199\t\n200\t Waveform encoder \n201\t\n202\t We use a custom 1-D ResNet-18 to map a single-channel waveform segment of length L L (the number of raw audio samples) to a temporal feature map: \n203\t\n204\t 𝐄 A : ℝ L → ℝ C e × T , T = ⌊ L / 32 ⌋ , mathbf{E}_{A}:mathbb{R}^{L}tomathbb{R}^{C_{e}times T},qquad T=lfloor L/32rfloor, \n205\t\n206\t (1) \n207\t\n208\t where C e C_{e} is the number of feature channels the encoder produces at each time step and T T is the resulting sequence length (the number of temporal feature vectors handed to the aggregation module, see Section 3.4 ). The 32 32 -fold downsampling from L L to T T accumulates over a stride-2 stem (conv  7 × 1 7{times}1 + + max-pool) followed by three stride-2 residual stages. The values of C e C_{e} , L L , or T T are not intrinsic to the fusion mechanism: C e = 512 C_{e}=512 reflects the standard ResNet-18 backbone, L = 6 , 400 L=6{,}400 results from sampling a raw audio waveform at 16 16  kHz, and the sampling rate (400 ms) was chosen to match the typical duration of a single vocalization event in our target datasets. Employing a different backbone, segment length, or sampling rate would rescale these values without altering the underlying uncertainty-aware fusion method. \n209\t\n210\t Spectrogram encoder \n211\t\n212\t We use a standard ResNet-18 (He et al. , 2016 ) to produce frequency features: 𝐄 F : ℝ C × H × W → ℝ C e × T mathbf{E}_{F}:mathbb{R}^{Ctimes Htimes W}tomathbb{R}^{C_{e}times T} . This means that the backbone maps ( B , C , H , W ) → ( B , C e , H / 32 , W / 32 ) (B,C,H,W)to(B,C_{e},H/32,W/32) , then an average pooling collapses the frequency axis, and linear interpolation aligns the temporal axis to T T . With H = W = 128 H=W=128 the backbone yields a 4 × 4 4times 4 spatial map after frequency pooling. There are four distinct temporal observations, which are linearly interpolated to T = 200 T=200 to match the waveform encoder’s output shape ( B , C e , T ) (B,C_{e},T) . \n213\t\n214\t 3.4 Temporal Context Aggregation \n215\t\n216\t Each encoder sequence { e m , t } t = 1 T ⊂ ℝ C e {e_{m,t}}_{t=1}^{T}subsetmathbb{R}^{C_{e}} is aggregated into a single utterance-level embedding 𝐡 m ∈ ℝ D h mathbf{h}_{m}inmathbb{R}^{D_{h}} . To yield this embedding, our proposed UAF model applies global average pooling: \n217\t\n218\t 𝐡 m = 1 T ​ ∑ t = 1 T e m , t , D h = C e = 512 . mathbf{h}_{m}=frac{1}{T}sum_{t=1}^{T}e_{m,t},qquad D_{h}=C_{e}=512. \n219\t\n220\t (2) \n221\t\n222\t We additionally evaluate three alternative aggregation modules as ablations of this design choice: \n223\t\n224\t 1. \n225\t\n226\t GRU : A multi-layer GRU processes the sequence in temporal order, and 𝐡 m mathbf{h}_{m} is taken as the final hidden state of the last recurrent layer. \n227\t\n228\t 2. \n229\t\n230\t Transformer : A Pre-Layer Normalization Transformer encoder with learnable positional embeddings operates on the time step sequence, and the output is mean-pooled to 𝐡 m ∈ ℝ C e mathbf{h}_{m}inmathbb{R}^{C_{e}} . \n231\t\n232\t 3. \n233\t\n234\t Attention pooling : A two-layer MLP processes the embeddings of each time step, and a softmax over the sequence turns the output into attention weights such that the time steps that are more informative contribute more. \n235\t\n236\t For all variants, 𝐡 m mathbf{h}_{m} is further processed by the downstream uncertainty head and fusion module, isolating temporal aggregation as the critical architectural design in the ablation. \n237\t\n238\t 3.5 Gaussian Uncertainty Parameterization \n239\t\n240\t The gaussian uncertainty parameterization is the major part where our approach differs from a conventional dual-stream classifier. Instead of treating each modality’s utterance embedding 𝐡 m mathbf{h}_{m} as a fixed point, an uncertainty head maps it to a distribution, producing a mean 𝝁 m boldsymbol{mu}_{m} and a variance 𝝈 m 2 boldsymbol{sigma}_{m}^{2} . The mean plays the role of a conventional embedding, while the variance indicates the model’s confidence in that estimate. Therefore, the variance should be small along dimensions where a modality is clear and unambiguous, and it should be large along dimensions where the input is noisy or degraded. As there are no reliability labels, the association between uncertainty and input quality is learned using a loss function inspired by (Tellamekala et al. , 2023 ) and (Xie et al. , 2024b ) . We explain the loss function and its empirical evaluation in Sections 3.7 and 5.3 , respectively. \n241\t\n242\t We use two independent linear layers to yield 𝝁 m boldsymbol{mu}_{m} and 𝝈 m boldsymbol{sigma}_{m} from the embeddings of the above-described waveform and spectrogram encoders: \n243\t\n244\t 𝝁 m = W μ ( m ) ​ 𝐡 m , 𝝈 m = softplus ​ ( W σ ( m ) ​ 𝐡 m ) + ε , boldsymbol{mu}_{m}=W_{mu}^{(m)}mathbf{h}_{m},qquadboldsymbol{sigma}_{m}=mathrm{softplus}!left(W_{sigma}^{(m)}mathbf{h}_{m}right)+varepsilon, \n245\t\n246\t (3) \n247\t\n248\t where W μ ( m ) , W σ ( m ) ∈ ℝ D z × D h W_{mu}^{(m)},W_{sigma}^{(m)}inmathbb{R}^{D_{z}times D_{h}} are learnable projections. Softplus keeps 𝝈 m boldsymbol{sigma}_{m} positive while remaining differentiable everywhere, such that it can be trained end-to-end with standard backpropagation. Adding the small constant ε = 10 − 6 varepsilon=10^{-6} guarantees 𝝈 m boldsymbol{sigma}_{m} never reaches exactly zero, which protects the fusion rule given in Section 3.6 from dividing by zero. We consid\n249\t\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "ac020b21-6746-4c7c-b94a-3a5b96c32d4d", "uuid": "316ab793-f238-4f69-a046-a3816646cae3", "timestamp": "2026-08-04T07:31:18.676Z", "tool_use_result": {"type": "text", "file": {"filePath": "/inspire/qb-ilm/project/video-generation/chenxie-25019/hyr/iHateTheWorld555.github.io/.claude-review/projects/-inspire-qb-ilm-project-video-generation-chenxie-25019-hyr-iHateTheWorld555-github-io/ac020b21-6746-4c7c-b94a-3a5b96c32d4d/tool-results/b1i6c339s.txt", "content": " \n\n Uncertainty-Aware Crossmodal Fusion for Classification of Animal Behavior \n\n Report GitHub Issue \n × \n\n Title: \n\n Content selection saved. Describe the issue below: \n\n Description: \n\n Submit without GitHub \n Submit in GitHub \n\n arXiv is now an independent nonprofit! \n Learn more \n &times; \n\n Back to arXiv \n\n Why HTML? \n\n Report Issue \n\n Back to Abstract \n\n Download PDF \n\n Abstract \n 1 Introduction \n\n 2 Related Work \n\n 2.1 Acoustic Representations in Animal Bioacoustics \n 2.2 Spectrogram-based Deep Learning Models \n 2.3 Raw Waveform and Hybrid Audio Models \n 2.4 Multimodal Fusion Strategies \n 2.5 Uncertainty and Reliability Modeling in Multimodal Learning \n\n 3 Methods \n\n 3.1 Problem Definition \n 3.2 Architecture Overview \n\n 3.3 Dual Audio Encoder \n\n Waveform encoder \n Spectrogram encoder \n\n 3.4 Temporal Context Aggregation \n 3.5 Gaussian Uncertainty Parameterization \n 3.6 Dimension-wise Uncertainty Fusion \n\n 3.7 Training Objective \n\n Classification loss \n Difficulty-Confidence loss \n Variance regularizer \n\n 4 Experimental Setup \n\n 4.1 Datasets and Data Splits \n\n SoundWel Dataset \n DogBark Dataset \n Data Splits \n\n 4.2 Preprocessing and Data Augmentation \n\n Audio Preprocessing \n Temporal Segmentation \n Data Augmentation \n\n 4.3 Training Procedure \n\n Stage 1: Hyperparameter Optimization \n Stage 2: Final Model Training \n\n 4.4 Test-Time Inference and Segment Aggregation \n 4.5 Implementation Details \n\n 5 Results and Discussion \n\n 5.1 Quantitative Performance Comparison \n\n Role of temporal architecture \n\n 5.2 Per-Class Classification Analysis \n 5.3 Prediction Calibration \n 5.4 Prediction Confidence by Behavioral Context \n 5.5 Fusion Weight Adaptation Under Targeted Modality Corruption \n\n 6 Conclusion \n References \n A Complete SoundWel Confusion Matrix \n B Random Stratified Split Results \n\n License: arXiv.org perpetual non-exclusive license\n\narXiv:2608.02104v1 [cs.SD] 03 Aug 2026 \n\n Uncertainty-Aware Crossmodal Fusion for Classification of Animal Behavior \n\n Ehsan Yaghoubi\n\n ehsan.yaghoubi@hswt.de \n\n Florian Haselbeck\n\n florian.haselbeck@hswt.de \n\n Abstract \n Artificial intelligence offers substantial potential for acoustic monitoring of animals, from welfare assessment in precision livestock farming to wildlife conservation and ecological research, where vocalizations can indicate health, stress, and social states earlier and at lower cost than manual observation. However, recordings in these settings are obtained under uncontrolled conditions, including environmental noise, reverberation, overlapping calls, and sensors that degrade without notice. As a consequence, automated classification of animal vocalizations remains challenging, and the two dominant acoustic representations show complementary limitations: raw waveforms preserve temporal microstructure but degrade under clipping and reverberation, while log-Mel spectrograms capture harmonic organization but lose phase information and are sensitive to broadband noise. To address these challenges, we propose Uncertainty-Aware Fusion (UAF), a dual-stream framework that estimates Gaussian uncertainty for each representation and fuses them via uncertainty weighting. This mechanism assigns greater weight to the more confident representation with no reliability labels required. In a cross-species, identity-based evaluation excluding all individuals seen during training, UAF (mean pooling) achieves 59.4% accuracy / 39.7% macro F1 on the 17-class SoundWel pig vocalization benchmark and 73.1% accuracy / 71.5% macro F1 on the 3-class DogBark dataset, outperforming static-concatenation fusion by 15.7% and 20.4% relative macro F1, respectively. Ablations over four temporal aggregation strategies show that uncertainty fusion, rather than the temporal characteristics of animal calls, is the primary driver of the performance gain. \n\n keywords: \nUncertainty Analysis , Multimodal Fusion , Bioacoustics , Animal Welfare , Precision Livestock Farming.\n\n † † journal: Computers and Electronics in Agriculture \n\n affiliation \n [label1]organization=Weihenstephan-Triesdorf University of Applied Sciences,addressline=Department of Sustainable Agricultural and Energy Systems,\ncity=Freising,\nstreet=Am Staudengarten 1,,\npostcode=85354,\ncountry=Germany\n\n 1 Introduction \n\n Precision livestock farming employs sensor-based monitoring to assess health, stress, and welfare, often at the level of the individual animal (Berckmans, 2017 ) . Acoustic sensing is a widely used modality, since it requires no animal contact, covers a pen from a fixed position, and adds comparatively little hardware per monitored unit (Coutant et al. , 2024 ; Shorten and Welten, 2021 ; Shorten and Hunter, 2023 ) . Vocalizations have furthermore been associated with pain, thermal discomfort, hunger, and social conflict (Manteuffel et al. , 2004 ) . In recent years, deep learning-based approaches trained on raw audio or spectrogram input have reached high accuracy on curated datasets, in welfare assessment (Coutant et al. , 2024 ) as well as in wildlife conservation (Kershenbaum et al. , 2025 ) and ecological research (Penar et al. , 2020 ) . This performance is largely obtained under favorable acquisition conditions and is often not reproduced in deployment, where signal quality is a limiting factor: recordings contain machinery and environmental noise, reverberation from hard surfaces, and overlapping calls from multiple animals, and sensors degrade gradually without this being detected at the time of recording. Since such predictions inform subsequent management decisions, quantifying the reliability of a prediction is of interest in addition to classification performance. \n\n Generally, there are two dominant acoustic representations, namely raw time-domain waveforms and log-Mel frequency spectrograms. While processing waveform signals preserves temporal microstructure and phase, it is sensitive to clipping and reverberation (Baevski et al. , 2020 ) . On the other hand, spectrogram-based models capture harmonic and frequency organization but discard phase and degrade under broadband noise (Stowell, 2022 ) . Therefore, neither representation is uniformly superior because the relative reliability shifts with recording conditions, which is a practically unavoidable reality in uncontrolled environments and field deployments. Existing dual-representation approaches (Kong et al. , 2020 ; Liang et al. , 2022 ) demonstrate that combining waveform and spectrogram features consistently outperforms either stream alone. However, these methods usually employ static fusion : both representations receive fixed weights regardless of their reliability for a given input. Under the variable acoustic conditions of real-world animal monitoring, static weights propagate noise from the degraded representation rather than suppressing it. Although uncertainty-aware fusion has been explored in audio-visual human emotion recognition (Tellamekala et al. , 2023 ; Xie et al. , 2024b ) , no prior work has adapted reliability-driven weighting to audio dual-representation fusion for bioacoustic classification. This is challenging because, unlike audio-visual fusion, both modalities originate from the same acoustic signal, requiring the model to determine which representation should be trusted in each step in time. \n\n We address this gap with Uncertainty-Aware Fusion (UAF) , a framework that dynamically weights waveform and spectrogram contributions using per-utterance latent uncertainty estimates (Fig.  1 ). First, modality-specific ResNet-18 encoders map each input to a temporal feature sequence, and a temporal aggregation module produces a single utterance-level embedding per modality. An uncertainty head then parameterizes a per-utterance diagonal Gaussian 𝒩 ​ ( 𝝁 m , 𝝈 m 2 ) mathcal{N}(boldsymbol{mu}_{m},boldsymbol{sigma}_{m}^{2}) for each modality m ∈ { A , F } min{A,F} , and dimension-wise uncertainty weights fuse the two means: each latent dimension is dominated by whichever modality is more confident for that input. Training optimizes a multi-term objective that learns from three streams, i.e., waveform, spectrogram, and a fusion branch. We evaluate on two publicly available bioacoustic benchmark datasets, i.e., SoundWel, a 17-class pig welfare vocalization (Briefer et al. , 2022 ) and DogBark, a dataset for context-dependent dog bark classification (Yin and McCowan, 2004 ) .\nIn summary, our contributions are threefold: \n\n 1. \n\n We propose a dual-representation architecture for bioacoustic classification that processes raw waveform and log-Mel spectrogram inputs in parallel, capturing temporal and frequency cues that neither representation provides on its own. \n\n 2. \n\n We employ an uncertainty-aware fusion mechanism that estimates the reliability of each representation at every time step and weights their contributions accordingly. This directly targets a practical problem in real-world and barn recordings, where noise, clipping, or occlusion can degrade one representation while leaving the other intact. \n\n 3. \n\n We evaluate our framework on two bioacoustic benchmarks, SoundWel (pig, 17 classes) and DogBark (dog, 3 classes), and report ablation studies that show the gain from uncertainty-aware fusion relative to static concatenation baselines. \n\n 2 Related Work \n\n 2.1 Acoustic Representations in Animal Bioacoustics \n\n The time-domain waveform and the frequency spectrogram are two different representations of acoustic signals that can be used for behavioral classification of animals. Early approaches relied on hand-crafted features for specific species and monitoring objectives (Coutant et al. , 2024 ; Mcloughlin et al. , 2019 ) . These features are grouped into three types: time-domain descriptors such as call duration and inter-call intervals; frequency-domain descriptors including fundamental frequency, formants, and spectral centroid; and energy-related measures such as amplitude and signal-to-noise ratio. Dynamic features such as jitter and shimmer capture temporal micro-variations, while derived representations such as Mel-Frequency Cepstral Coefficients (MFCCs) and their temporal derivatives have become common in automatic classification systems (Li et al. , 2024 ) . For instance, pig distress calls exhibit higher pitch, longer duration, and noisier spectra, whereas chicken alarm calls are typically short, high-pitched bursts (Coutant et al. , 2024 ) . While hand-crafted features have enabled considerable progress in livestock monitoring (Mcloughlin et al. , 2019 ) and wildlife conservation, they require substantial domain expertise, may not generalize well across recording conditions or species, and can miss subtle, nonlinear interactions between temporal and spectral information. These limitations have motivated a shift toward learned representations via deep neural networks; however, whether time-domain waveforms, frequency-domain spectrograms, or both yield the best learned features remains an open question with distinct trade-offs in information preservation, computational cost, and noise robustness. \n\n 2.2 Spectrogram-based Deep Learning Models \n\n Spectrogram-based approaches have emerged as the dominant paradigm in bioacoustic deep learning, converting raw audio signals into time-frequency representations such as mel-spectrograms, MFCCs, or delta-mel features before applying, e.g., convolutional neural networks (CNNs) (Stowell, 2022 ; Xie et al. , 2024a ) . By treating spectrograms as images, computer vision approaches can learn hierarchical spatial patterns that capture both temporal and spectral dynamics, consistently outperforming traditional classifiers based on hand-crafted features in comparative studies (Ghani et al. , 2023 ; Bergler et al. , 2022 ) . Hybrid architectures that incorporate recurrent units, e.g., CNN–LSTM or CNN–GRU, further enhance performance by modeling temporal dependencies across spectrogram frames (Gupta et al. , 2021 ) . More recently, transformer-based models that treat the log-mel spectrogram as a sequence of 2-D patches, such as the Audio Spectrogram Transformer (AST) (Gong et al. , 2021 ) , achieve state-of-the-art results on large-scale audio benchmarks. The AVES (Animal Vocalization Encoder based on Self-Supervision) model (Hagiwara, 2023 ) extends this paradigm to animal vocalizations via self-supervised transformer pre-training, yielding strong transfer performance on bioacoustic classification. \n\n These spectrogram-based models have been successfully applied across diverse bioacoustic domains. In livestock applications, automated classification systems using multiple CNN backbones (e.g., AlexNet, ResNet, EfficientNet) have demonstrated high accuracy for dog vocalizations (Gómez-Armenta et al. , 2024 ) , while emotion recognition systems show promise for farm animal welfare monitoring (Briefer et al. , 2022 ) . For wildlife monitoring, large-scale species classification of birds and insects has leveraged CNNs to capture discriminative frequency patterns across taxa (Nanni et al. , 2020 ; Stowell, 2022 ) , while in marine bioacoustics, deep learning models have been developed for detecting baleen whale vocalizations (Schall et al. , 2024 ) . Recent frameworks such as ANIMAL-SPOT (Bergler et al. , 2022 ) provide end-to-end pipelines for detection and classification directly from spectrograms, showing strong generalization across multiple species and call types. \n\n Despite these empirical successes, spectrogram-based methods have known limitations. The time-frequency transformation inherently discards phase information, which may contain discriminative temporal microstructures relevant for certain call types. Model performance is sensitive to spectrogram resolution and preprocessing choices such as window size, hop length, and frequency binning, requiring careful tuning for each application. Furthermore, performance often degrades when deployed in real-world scenarios that differ from training conditions, particularly under varying noise profiles or recording equipment (Stowell et al. , 2019 ; Shorten and Hunter, 2023 ) . These robustness challenges suggest that relying solely on frequency-domain representations could be insufficient for generalizable bioacoustic monitoring systems. \n\n 2.3 Raw Waveform and Hybrid Audio Models \n\n Motivated by the potential information loss in spectrogram conversion, raw waveform modeling has gained attention as a complementary representation strategy. Raw waveform approaches often apply convolutional filters directly to time-domain audio signals, allowing the network to learn discriminative temporal features without explicit frequency transformation (Tokozume and Harada, 2017 ) . Self-supervised pre-training on raw waveforms—exemplified by wav2vec 2.0 (Baevski et al. , 2020 ) and HuBERT (Hsu et al. , 2021 ) —has demonstrated that contextual representations learned from large unlabeled audio corpora transfer effectively to downstream classification tasks. Large-scale pretrained models such as PANNs (Pretrained Audio Neural Networks) further show that fusing waveform- and spectrogram-level features improves audio pattern recognition. The Wavegram-Logmel-CNN variant in PANNs concatenates features from both domains and achieves better performance than either branch alone (Kong et al. , 2020 ) . The theoretical advantage of waveform models lies in preserving complete signal information, including phase relationships and fine-grained temporal structures that may be smoothed or lost during spectrogram computation. \n\n In bioacoustics, however, raw waveform modeling remains relatively underexplored compared to spectrogram-based approaches. Bravo Sanchez et al. demonstrate that SincNet-based waveform architectures can classify avian calls without spectrograms, establishing the viability of end-to-end waveform learning for bioacoustic tasks (Bravo Sanchez et al. , 2021 ) . Nevertheless, bioacoustic applications continue to face unique challenges, including data scarcity, high intra-species variability, and diverse recording conditions. More critically, although both representations offer complementary information, existing multi-representation approaches such as PANNs (Kong et al. , 2020 ) as well as the dual-stream waveform and log-mel fusion of Liang et al. ( 2022 ) rely on static, fixed-weight concatenation that does not adapt when one representation becomes less reliable under specific conditions. The key unresolved challenge is not whether to combine both representations but how to dynamically assess and respond to their relative reliability. \n\n 2.4 Multimodal Fusion Strategies \n\n The recognition that single representations may be insufficient has led to growing interest in multimodal integration, both across different sensor types and representations of the same audio signal. Baltrušaitis et al. provide the foundational taxonomy of multimodal learning strategies, distinguishing early fusion, late fusion, and intermediate fusion, and highlighting representation learning and fusion as the two central challenges (Baltrušaitis et al. , 2019 ) . In livestock monitoring, recent systems have combined acoustic signals with accelerometry and inertial sensors to improve the classification of behavioral states and welfare indicators (Vu et al. , 2024 ; Ferrero et al. , 2025 ) . These multimodal systems leverage complementary information from different sensing modalities to provide more robust predictions in noisy farm environments, where visual occlusion, acoustic interference, or sensor failures can degrade individual channels. \n\n However, most existing fusion strategies employ simple integration methods that assume equal reliability across modalities. Early fusion approaches concatenate raw features or low-level representations before classification, while late fusion combines independent predictions from modality-specific classifiers through averaging or voting. Intermediate strategies extract separate embeddings from each modality and concatenate them before the final classification layers (Baltrušaitis et al. , 2019 ) . These methods treat all modalities equally informative at all times, assigning fixed or learned but static weights that do not adapt to temporal variations in signal quality. Peng et al. show empirically that joint multimodal training suffers from a modality-imbalance problem and propose on-the-fly gradient modulation as a solution (Peng et al. , 2022 ) . \n\n This assumption of uniform reliability is problematic in real-world deployments, where waveform and spectrogram representations show input-dependent failure modes. Existing fusion methods amplify the noise from a degraded representation rather than suppress it, motivating reliability-adaptive weighting. \n\n 2.5 Uncertainty and Reliability Modeling in Multimodal Learning \n\n The concept of uncertainty-aware fusion, where modality contributions are dynamically weighted based on estimated reliability, has roots in Bayesian deep learning. Gal and Ghahramani showed that Monte Carlo dropout provides a computationally tractable approximation to Bayesian inference, yielding epistemic uncertainty estimates at test time without modifying the training procedure (Gal and Ghahramani, 2016 ) , while Kendall and Gal formalized the canonical decomposition into aleatoric uncertainty (irreducible data noise) and epistemic uncertainty (model uncertainty reducible with more data) (Kendall and Gal, 2017 ) . In the multimodal domain, Subedar et al. ( 2019 ) applied deep Bayesian variational inference to audio-visual activity recognition and demonstrated that per-modality uncertainty estimates improve robustness when one sensor stream is corrupted. Han et al. ( 2023 ) extended uncertainty-driven fusion to arbitrary numbers of views via Dempster-Shafer evidence fusion, enabling reliable classification under missing or degraded inputs. In multimodal emotion recognition, the COLD Fusion framework (Tellamekala et al. , 2023 ) introduced a principled approach by learning latent distributions over unimodal temporal contexts and constraining their variance to represent how informative each modality is toward the prediction task. By imposing calibration and ordinal ranking constraints on modality-wise uncertainty estimates, COLD Fusion enables adaptive weighting that favors more reliable modalities at each time step. \n\n 3 Methods \n\n The core idea of the proposed Uncertainty-Aware Fusion (UAF) framework is to replace static fusion weights for time-domain waveform and spectrogram representations with adaptive weights estimated directly from the learned features. This enables the model to quantify modality-specific uncertainty and suppress less reliable representations without requiring explicit reliability annotations. We first formulate the classification task and establish the notation used throughout the paper. We then describe the proposed framework in detail, including the overall architecture, unimodal waveform and spectrogram encoders, temporal aggregation, uncertainty estimation, the adaptive fusion strategy, and the corresponding training objective. \n\n 3.1 Problem Definition \n\n The task we address in this paper is to classify animal vocalizations into K K discrete behavioral categories. Given an audio recording, two complementary representations are extracted: a raw waveform x A ∈ ℝ L x_{A}inmathbb{R}^{L} and a three-channel stacked spectrogram x F ∈ ℝ C × H × W x_{F}inmathbb{R}^{Ctimes Htimes W} , where L L is the waveform length, C = 3 C=3 channels encode log-Mel, Δ Delta -Mel, and Δ ​ Δ DeltaDelta -Mel coefficients, and H × W Htimes W is the spectrogram spatial resolution (Young et al. , 2002 ; Takahashi et al. , 2016 ) . We consider three-channel spectrogram input rather than the one-channel log-Mel because the derivative channels capture short-term spectral dynamics (onset, offset, and formant-transition cues) that the log-Mel spectrogram discards (Young et al. , 2002 ) . The goal is to learn a mapping f : ( x A , x F ) ↦ y ^ ∈ { 1 , … , K } f:(x_{A},x_{F})mapstohat{y}in{1,ldots,K} that minimizes classification error while producing calibrated confidence estimates. \n\n 3.2 Architecture Overview \n\n Figure  1 illustrates the proposed Uncertainty-Aware Fusion (UAF) framework, which we realize in four steps. First, we perform modality encoding by passing each modality through a dedicated ResNet-18 encoder, producing a temporal sequence of T T feature vectors of dimension C e = 512 C_{e}=512 . These features are compressed by a temporal aggregation module into a single utterance-level embedding 𝐡 m ∈ ℝ C e mathbf{h}_{m}inmathbb{R}^{C_{e}} (see Sections 3.3 and 3.4 ). Then, we perform uncertainty parameterization by mapping each embedding to the mean and variance of a diagonal Gaussian, q m ​ ( z ∣ 𝐡 m ) = 𝒩 ​ ( 𝝁 m , diag ​ ( 𝝈 m 2 ) ) q_{m}(zmidmathbf{h}_{m})=mathcal{N}(boldsymbol{mu}_{m},mathrm{diag}(boldsymbol{sigma}_{m}^{2})) for m ∈ A , F min{A,F} , where A A and F F denote the waveform and spectrogram modalities. We use the predicted variance as an implicit measure of modality reliability, enabling the model to adaptively weight each modality without requiring explicit reliability annotations (see Section 3.5 ). Next, in uncertainty-weighted fusion step, we combine these Gaussians dimension-wise, giving more weight to whichever modality has lower variance in each latent dimension, and obtain a fused latent sample 𝐳 ∈ ℝ D z mathbf{z}inmathbb{R}^{D_{z}} (see Section 3.6 ). Finally, we do a joint classification by applying a linear classifier to fused representation 𝐳 mathbf{z} to obtain the final prediction, while two auxiliary classifiers are applied to 𝝁 A boldsymbol{mu}_{A} and 𝝁 F boldsymbol{mu}_{F} to provide modality-specific supervision. We train the whole model with a loss function inspired by (Tellamekala et al. , 2023 ) and (Xie et al. , 2024b ) that couples the predicted variances with the observed classification difficulty (see Section 3.7 ). \n\n Figure 1: Overview of the proposed Uncertainty-Aware Fusion (UAF) framework for animal vocalization classification. Audio waveform and frequency specgrogram are encoded separately and temporally aggregated. The resulting embeddings are mapped to Gaussian means and variances, and the uncertainty-aware fusion module combines the two modalities into a single fused distribution by assigning higher weights to the more certain modality. A vector sampled from this fused distribution, together with two auxiliary unimodal predictions, is used for the final classification. A difficulty-confidence loss couples each modality’s uncertainty to how hard its predictions are, while a regularization term keeps the uncertainties feasible for an end-to-end train. \n\n 3.3 Dual Audio Encoder \n\n We use separate encoders for the raw waveform and the spectrogram: \n\n Waveform encoder \n\n We use a custom 1-D ResNet-18 to map a single-channel waveform segment of length L L (the number of raw audio samples) to a temporal feature map: \n\n 𝐄 A : ℝ L → ℝ C e × T , T = ⌊ L / 32 ⌋ , mathbf{E}_{A}:mathbb{R}^{L}tomathbb{R}^{C_{e}times T},qquad T=lfloor L/32rfloor, \n\n (1) \n\n where C e C_{e} is the number of feature channels the encoder produces at each time step and T T is the resulting sequence length (the number of temporal feature vectors handed to the aggregation module, see Section 3.4 ). The 32 32 -fold downsampling from L L to T T accumulates over a stride-2 stem (conv  7 × 1 7{times}1 + + max-pool) followed by three stride-2 residual stages. The values of C e C_{e} , L L , or T T are not intrinsic to the fusion mechanism: C e = 512 C_{e}=512 reflects the standard ResNet-18 backbone, L = 6 , 400 L=6{,}400 results from sampling a raw audio waveform at 16 16  kHz, and the sampling rate (400 ms) was chosen to match the typical duration of a single vocalization event in our target datasets. Employing a different backbone, segment length, or sampling rate would rescale these values without altering the underlying uncertainty-aware fusion method. \n\n Spectrogram encoder \n\n We use a standard ResNet-18 (He et al. , 2016 ) to produce frequency features: 𝐄 F : ℝ C × H × W → ℝ C e × T mathbf{E}_{F}:mathbb{R}^{Ctimes Htimes W}tomathbb{R}^{C_{e}times T} . This means that the backbone maps ( B , C , H , W ) → ( B , C e , H / 32 , W / 32 ) (B,C,H,W)to(B,C_{e},H/32,W/32) , then an average pooling collapses the frequency axis, and linear interpolation aligns the temporal axis to T T . With H = W = 128 H=W=128 the backbone yields a 4 × 4 4times 4 spatial map after frequency pooling. There are four distinct temporal observations, which are linearly interpolated to T = 200 T=200 to match the waveform encoder’s output shape ( B , C e , T ) (B,C_{e},T) . \n\n 3.4 Temporal Context Aggregation \n\n Each encoder sequence { e m , t } t = 1 T ⊂ ℝ C e {e_{m,t}}_{t=1}^{T}subsetmathbb{R}^{C_{e}} is aggregated into a single utterance-level embedding 𝐡 m ∈ ℝ D h mathbf{h}_{m}inmathbb{R}^{D_{h}} . To yield this embedding, our proposed UAF model applies global average pooling: \n\n 𝐡 m = 1 T ​ ∑ t = 1 T e m , t , D h = C e = 512 . mathbf{h}_{m}=frac{1}{T}sum_{t=1}^{T}e_{m,t},qquad D_{h}=C_{e}=512. \n\n (2) \n\n We additionally evaluate three alternative aggregation modules as ablations of this design choice: \n\n 1. \n\n GRU : A multi-layer GRU processes the sequence in temporal order, and 𝐡 m mathbf{h}_{m} is taken as the final hidden state of the last recurrent layer. \n\n 2. \n\n Transformer : A Pre-Layer Normalization Transformer encoder with learnable positional embeddings operates on the time step sequence, and the output is mean-pooled to 𝐡 m ∈ ℝ C e mathbf{h}_{m}inmathbb{R}^{C_{e}} . \n\n 3. \n\n Attention pooling : A two-layer MLP processes the embeddings of each time step, and a softmax over the sequence turns the output into attention weights such that the time steps that are more informative contribute more. \n\n For all variants, 𝐡 m mathbf{h}_{m} is further processed by the downstream uncertainty head and fusion module, isolating temporal aggregation as the critical architectural design in the ablation. \n\n 3.5 Gaussian Uncertainty Parameterization \n\n The gaussian uncertainty parameterization is the major part where our approach differs from a conventional dual-stream classifier. Instead of treating each modality’s utterance embedding 𝐡 m mathbf{h}_{m} as a fixed point, an uncertainty head maps it to a distribution, producing a mean 𝝁 m boldsymbol{mu}_{m} and a variance 𝝈 m 2 boldsymbol{sigma}_{m}^{2} . The mean plays the role of a conventional embedding, while the variance indicates the model’s confidence in that estimate. Therefore, the variance should be small along dimensions where a modality is clear and unambiguous, and it should be large along dimensions where the input is noisy or degraded. As there are no reliability labels, the association between uncertainty and input quality is learned using a loss function inspired by (Tellamekala et al. , 2023 ) and (Xie et al. , 2024b ) . We explain the loss function and its empirical evaluation in Sections 3.7 and 5.3 , respectively. \n\n We use two independent linear layers to yield 𝝁 m boldsymbol{mu}_{m} and 𝝈 m boldsymbol{sigma}_{m} from the embeddings of the above-described waveform and spectrogram encoders: \n\n 𝝁 m = W μ ( m ) ​ 𝐡 m , 𝝈 m = softplus ​ ( W σ ( m ) ​ 𝐡 m ) + ε , boldsymbol{mu}_{m}=W_{mu}^{(m)}mathbf{h}_{m},qquadboldsymbol{sigma}_{m}=mathrm{softplus}!left(W_{sigma}^{(m)}mathbf{h}_{m}right)+varepsilon, \n\n (3) \n\n where W μ ( m ) , W σ ( m ) ∈ ℝ D z × D h W_{mu}^{(m)},W_{sigma}^{(m)}inmathbb{R}^{D_{z}times D_{h}} are learnable projections. Softplus keeps 𝝈 m boldsymbol{sigma}_{m} positive while remaining differentiable everywhere, such that it can be trained end-to-end with standard backpropagation. Adding the small constant ε = 10 − 6 varepsilon=10^{-6} guarantees 𝝈 m boldsymbol{sigma}_{m} never reaches exactly zero, which protects the fusion rule given in Section 3.6 from dividing by zero. We consid\n", "numLines": 249, "startLine": 1, "totalLines": 249}}}
