{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-a6341707652c4e7c", "type": "tool_result", "content": "1\ter the dimensionality of 𝝁 m boldsymbol{mu}_{m} and 𝝈 m boldsymbol{sigma}_{m} as a hyperparameter ( D z D_{z} ) and optimize it using Bayesian optimization leveraging the Python framework Optuna (Akiba et al. , 2019 ) (see Section 4.3 ). \n2\t\n3\t 3.6 Dimension-wise Uncertainty Fusion \n4\t\n5\t Given the Gaussian parameters ( 𝝁 A , 𝝈 A ) (boldsymbol{mu}_{A},boldsymbol{sigma}_{A}) and ( 𝝁 F , 𝝈 F ) (boldsymbol{mu}_{F},boldsymbol{sigma}_{F}) , fusion weights are computed independently for each latent dimension d ∈ { 1 , … , D z } din{1,ldots,D_{z}} : \n6\t\n7\t w A ​ ( d ) = σ F ​ ( d ) σ A ​ ( d ) + σ F ​ ( d ) , w F ​ ( d ) = σ A ​ ( d ) σ A ​ ( d ) + σ F ​ ( d ) . w_{A}(d)=frac{sigma_{F}(d)}{sigma_{A}(d)+sigma_{F}(d)},qquad w_{F}(d)=frac{sigma_{A}(d)}{sigma_{A}(d)+sigma_{F}(d)}. \n8\t\n9\t (4) \n10\t\n11\t Consequently, a dimension in which the spectrogram is highly uncertain ( σ F ​ ( d ) ≫ σ A ​ ( d ) sigma_{F}(d)ggsigma_{A}(d) ) receives proportionally more weight from the waveform, and vice versa. Stacking these per-dimension weights into vectors 𝐰 A , 𝐰 F ∈ ℝ D z mathbf{w}_{A},mathbf{w}_{F}inmathbb{R}^{D_{z}} (with 𝐰 A + 𝐰 F = 𝟏 mathbf{w}_{A}+mathbf{w}_{F}=mathbf{1} ), the fused Gaussian parameters are defined as: \n12\t\n13\t 𝝁 z displaystyleboldsymbol{mu}_{z} \n14\t = 𝐰 A ⊙ 𝝁 A + 𝐰 F ⊙ 𝝁 F , displaystyle=mathbf{w}_{A}odotboldsymbol{mu}_{A}+mathbf{w}_{F}odotboldsymbol{mu}_{F}, \n15\t\n16\t (5) \n17\t\n18\t 𝝈 z displaystyleboldsymbol{sigma}_{z} \n19\t = 𝐰 A ⊙ 𝝈 A + 𝐰 F ⊙ 𝝈 F . displaystyle=mathbf{w}_{A}odotboldsymbol{sigma}_{A}+mathbf{w}_{F}odotboldsymbol{sigma}_{F}. \n20\t\n21\t (6) \n22\t\n23\t During training, a latent sample is drawn via the reparameterization trick: \n24\t\n25\t 𝐳 = 𝝁 z + 𝝈 z ⊙ 𝜺 , 𝜺 ∼ 𝒩 ​ ( 𝟎 , 𝐈 ) . mathbf{z}=boldsymbol{mu}_{z}+boldsymbol{sigma}_{z}odotboldsymbol{varepsilon},quadboldsymbol{varepsilon}simmathcal{N}(mathbf{0},mathbf{I}). \n26\t\n27\t (7) \n28\t\n29\t At inference, we set 𝐳 = 𝝁 z mathbf{z}=boldsymbol{mu}_{z} . The vector 𝐳 mathbf{z} is then further processed by a dropout-regularized linear layer to produce class logits ℓ ^ z = W c ​ 𝐳 ∈ ℝ K hat{ell}_{z}=W_{c}mathbf{z}inmathbb{R}^{K} . \n30\t\n31\t 3.7 Training Objective \n32\t\n33\t Inspired by Tellamekala et al. ( 2023 ) and Xie et al. ( 2024b ) , we design a novel loss function that supervises classification from the fused and unimodal predictions, and simultaneously learns the uncertainty to help a dynamic fusion based on the confidence of the modalities. In the following, we explain each component of this loss function. \n34\t\n35\t Classification loss \n36\t\n37\t Each unimodal stream shown in Fig.  1 has an independent linear head on top of 𝝁 A boldsymbol{mu}_{A} and 𝝁 F boldsymbol{mu}_{F} , allowing it to learn the class label directly from that modality. The fused branch (logits from 𝐳 mathbf{z} ) is additionally supervised using the same class label. Together, the three branches form the classification loss: \n38\t\n39\t ℒ cls = 1 3 ​ [ ℋ ​ ( ℓ ^ z , y ) + ℋ ​ ( ℓ ^ A , y ) + ℋ ​ ( ℓ ^ F , y ) ] , mathcal{L}_{mathrm{cls}}=frac{1}{3}Bigl[mathcal{H}(hat{ell}_{z},,y)+mathcal{H}(hat{ell}_{A},,y)+mathcal{H}(hat{ell}_{F},,y)Bigr], \n40\t\n41\t (8) \n42\t\n43\t where ℋ ​ ( ⋅ , y ) mathcal{H}(cdot,y) denotes cross-entropy with the ground-truth label y y , and ℓ ^ m = W m ​ 𝝁 m hat{ell}_{m}=W_{m}boldsymbol{mu}_{m} . During training, ℓ ^ z hat{ell}_{z} is computed from the reparameterized sample, see Eq.  7 , while in the inference phase, the unimodal classifiers receive means 𝝁 A boldsymbol{mu}_{A} and 𝝁 F boldsymbol{mu}_{F} without the reparameterization noise. \n44\t\n45\t Difficulty-Confidence loss \n46\t\n47\t The fusion process requires uncertainty scores. However, no ground-truth labels for uncertainty are available. Therefore, we compare within each mini-batch of B B samples how hard a sample actually was to classify against how confident the model was about its prediction. This allows the model to learn meaningful uncertainty estimates from relative comparisons within a batch. \n48\t\n49\t For each modality m ∈ { A , F } min{A,F} and sample i i , we compute a difficulty score (the sample’s own cross-entropy loss) and a confidence score (the inverse of its predicted uncertainty): \n50\t\n51\t d m ( i ) = ℋ ​ ( ℓ ^ m ( i ) , y ( i ) ) , s m ( i ) = 1 ‖ 𝝈 m ( i ) ‖ 2 + ε . d_{m}^{(i)}=mathcal{H}(hat{ell}_{m}^{(i)},,y^{(i)}),qquad s_{m}^{(i)}=frac{1}{|boldsymbol{sigma}_{m}^{(i)}|_{2}+varepsilon}. \n52\t\n53\t (9) \n54\t\n55\t Both scores are then normalized via a softmax with temperature τ tau over all B B samples into two probability distributions describing how difficult and how confident each sample is relative to the rest of the batch : \n56\t\n57\t 𝐩 D m = softmax ​ ( 𝐝 m / τ ) , 𝐩 S m = softmax ​ ( 𝐬 m / τ ) . mathbf{p}_{D}^{m}=mathrm{softmax}!left(mathbf{d}_{m},/,tauright),qquadmathbf{p}_{S}^{m}=mathrm{softmax}!left(mathbf{s}_{m},/,tauright). \n58\t\n59\t (10) \n60\t\n61\t Matching these two distributions via a symmetric KL divergence encourages samples that rank as relatively difficult under 𝐩 D m mathbf{p}_{D}^{m} to also rank as relatively confident under 𝐩 S m mathbf{p}_{S}^{m} , which corresponds to lower 𝝈 m boldsymbol{sigma}_{m} . In contrast, easy samples are encouraged to have lower confidence (higher 𝝈 m boldsymbol{sigma}_{m} ). Thus, the loss couples relative difficulty to relative confidence. The results in Section 5.3 show the correlation between ‖ σ z ‖ |sigma_{z}| and the misclassification error. For each modality m m , this correlation is imposed with the symmetric KL divergence: \n62\t\n63\t ℒ DC , m = KL ​ ( 𝐩 D m ∥ 𝐩 S m ) + KL ​ ( 𝐩 S m ∥ 𝐩 D m ) , m ∈ { A , F } . mathcal{L}_{mathrm{DC},m}=mathrm{KL}!left(mathbf{p}_{D}^{m},|,mathbf{p}_{S}^{m}right)+mathrm{KL}!left(mathbf{p}_{S}^{m},|,mathbf{p}_{D}^{m}right),quad min{A,F}. \n64\t\n65\t (11) \n66\t\n67\t To apply the same idea jointly (as a single loss term) across both modalities, we interleave the modalities’ difficulty and confidence scores into 2 ​ B 2B -length vectors: \n68\t\n69\t 𝐝 A ​ F = [ d A ( 1 ) , d F ( 1 ) , … , d A ( B ) , d F ( B ) ] , 𝐬 A ​ F = [ s A ( 1 ) , s F ( 1 ) , … , s A ( B ) , s F ( B ) ] . mathbf{d}_{AF}=bigl[d_{A}^{(1)},,d_{F}^{(1)},,ldots,,d_{A}^{(B)},,d_{F}^{(B)}bigr],quadmathbf{s}_{AF}=bigl[s_{A}^{(1)},,s_{F}^{(1)},,ldots,,s_{A}^{(B)},,s_{F}^{(B)}bigr]. \n70\t\n71\t (12) \n72\t\n73\t The same symmetric KL penalty is then applied to this combined set of 2 ​ B 2B samples: \n74\t\n75\t ℒ DC , A ​ F = KL ​ ( 𝐩 D A ​ F ∥ 𝐩 S A ​ F ) + KL ​ ( 𝐩 S A ​ F ∥ 𝐩 D A ​ F ) . mathcal{L}_{mathrm{DC},AF}=mathrm{KL}!left(mathbf{p}_{D}^{AF},|,mathbf{p}_{S}^{AF}right)+mathrm{KL}!left(mathbf{p}_{S}^{AF},|,mathbf{p}_{D}^{AF}right). \n76\t\n77\t (13) \n78\t\n79\t Variance regularizer \n80\t\n81\t To prevent variance collapse (the model pushing 𝝈 m → 0 boldsymbol{sigma}_{m}to 0 to suppress noisy gradients), we penalize departure from an isotropic standard normal prior (Tellamekala et al. , 2023 ; Xie et al. , 2024b ) : \n82\t\n83\t ℒ R = ∑ m ∈ { A , F } KL ​ ( 𝒩 ​ ( 𝝁 m , 𝝈 m 2 ) ∥ 𝒩 ​ ( 𝟎 , 𝐈 ) ) = − 1 2 ​ ∑ m ∑ d = 1 D z [ 1 + log ⁡ σ m , d 2 − μ m , d 2 − σ m , d 2 ] , begin{split}mathcal{L}_{R}&=sum_{min{A,F}}mathrm{KL}!left(mathcal{N}(boldsymbol{mu}_{m},boldsymbol{sigma}_{m}^{2}),big|,mathcal{N}(mathbf{0},mathbf{I})right)\n84\t&=-frac{1}{2}sum_{m}sum_{d=1}^{D_{z}}Bigl[1+logsigma_{m,d}^{2}-mu_{m,d}^{2}-sigma_{m,d}^{2}Bigr],end{split} \n85\t\n86\t (14) \n87\t\n88\t averaged over the batch. \n89\t\n90\t The total training objective composed of the above-described components is finally formulated as: \n91\t\n92\t ℒ = λ cls ​ ℒ cls + λ DC , A ​ ℒ DC , A + λ DC , F ​ ℒ DC , F + λ DC , A ​ F ​ ℒ DC , A ​ F + λ R ​ ℒ R , mathcal{L}=lambda_{mathrm{cls}},mathcal{L}_{mathrm{cls}}+lambda_{mathrm{DC},A},mathcal{L}_{mathrm{DC},A}+lambda_{mathrm{DC},F},mathcal{L}_{mathrm{DC},F}+lambda_{mathrm{DC},AF},mathcal{L}_{mathrm{DC},AF}+lambda_{R},mathcal{L}_{R}, \n93\t\n94\t (15) \n95\t\n96\t where default weights are set empirically to λ cls = 1 lambda_{mathrm{cls}}=1 , λ DC , A = λ DC , F = λ DC , A ​ F = 10 − 3 lambda_{mathrm{DC},A}=lambda_{mathrm{DC},F}=lambda_{mathrm{DC},AF}=10^{-3} , λ R = 10 − 4 lambda_{R}=10^{-4} . \n97\t\n98\t 4 Experimental Setup \n99\t\n100\t 4.1 Datasets and Data Splits \n101\t\n102\t We evaluate the proposed uncertainty-aware fusion framework on two publicly available datasets representing (i) different animal vocalization classification tasks to assess cross-species generalization and (ii) different task complexities: pig calls and dog barks context classification. \n103\t\n104\t SoundWel Dataset \n105\t\n106\t The SoundWel dataset (Briefer et al. , 2022 ) is a multi-institutional collection of pig vocalizations under the SoundWel research program. It contains 6,887 labeled audio clips recorded by six independent research teams across Europe (ETHZ, FBN, IASPA, IASPB, IASPC, NMBU), covering three age groups (piglet, weaner, growing/finishing) and 17 behavioral context categories spanning welfare-negative states (e.g. isolation, castration, crushing), welfare-positive states (e.g. enriched, reunion), and nursing-related behaviors. The dataset is severely class-imbalanced: For instance, isolation accounts for 29.7% of samples, while surprise accounts for only 0.2%. Individual clip durations have a mean of 0.36 s and a maximum of 3.6 s. \n107\t\n108\t DogBark Dataset \n109\t\n110\t The DogBark dataset (Yin and McCowan, 2004 ) contains dog vocalizations from ten individual dogs spanning six breeds, annotated across three behavioral contexts: aggression (14.3%), contact (55.6%), and play (30.2%). The original dataset comprises 4,672 samples; however, only 693 labeled recordings are publicly available. \n111\t\n112\t Data Splits \n113\t\n114\t A random stratified split would cause the recordings of the same individual to appear in both training and test sets, encouraging the model to exploit individual-specific vocal fingerprints rather than context-discriminative features. This is known as data leakage between train and test sets, which unrealistically inflates the model’s performance during the test phase (Bernett et al. , 2024 ) . Instead, in this work, we considered identity-aware splits for both datasets. However, results under a random stratified split are also provided in Table  1 ( B ) for comparison with the previously published result of Briefer et al. ( 2022 ) . \n115\t\n116\t The SoundWel dataset does not provide an identity train-val-test split, and only a limited number of samples have a pig ID. To avoid data leakage, we used the available metadata, i.e., filename patterns encoding per-animal or per-group identifiers (pen IDs, animal IDs, sow IDs, or litter numbers depending on team), recording year to disambiguate cohorts across experimental sessions, and recording team as a coarse grouping variable to prevent cross-team leakage. Recordings for which no parseable identity could be inferred (2,398 recordings, 34.8% of the dataset) are assigned to the training set such that the model could benefit from learning on more samples without the risk of data leakage. The resulting split is: train 5,452 (79.2%) | | val 677 (9.8%) | | test 758 (11.0%). All six recording teams contribute to every subset, and 13 of the 17 classes appear in the test set. The four absent classes in the test set (BeforeNursing, Fighting, Huddling, Run) have no recordings with parseable pig identities. Therefore, the macro-averaged F1-score is computed over the 13 classes present in the test partition. \n117\t\n118\t For the DogBark dataset, we ensure that individual dogs are assigned exclusively to one partition. The test set consists of Mac (German shorthaired pointer, male) and Zoe (Australian cattle dog, female), providing cross-breed and cross-sex generalization with all three behavioral classes represented. The validation set consists of Keri and Louie; the remaining six dogs form the training set. This data split leads to 458 train (66.1%), 75 val (10.8%), and 160 test (23.1%) samples. The class distribution is nearly identical across partitions, making the macro-averaged F1-score a fair evaluation metric. \n119\t\n120\t 4.2 Preprocessing and Data Augmentation \n121\t\n122\t Audio Preprocessing \n123\t\n124\t All audio recordings are resampled to 16 kHz sampling rate, which captures the relevant acoustic bandwidth for both pig and dog vocalizations (predominantly below 8 kHz) while reducing computational cost compared to higher sampling rates. This sampling rate aligns with standard practices in animal bioacoustics and enables stable training (Stowell, 2022 ) . \n125\t\n126\t For the waveform input ( x A x_{A} ), raw audio signals are peak-normalized by dividing by the absolute maximum amplitude, mapping each clip to a range of [ − 1 , 1 ] [-1,1] . For the spectrogram input ( x F x_{F} ), we compute a three-channel time-frequency representation using a Mel-filterbank applied to the power spectrogram (STFT with FFT size n fft = 1024 n_{text{fft}}=1024 , hop length = 256 =256 samples (16 ms), Hann window, and power = 2 =2 ). We extract 128 Mel-frequency bands spanning 0–8 kHz and convert to decibels (dB) to obtain the log-power Mel spectrogram. We then compute its first-order ( Δ Delta -Mel) and second-order temporal derivative ( Δ ​ Δ DeltaDelta -Mel). These three representations are stacked as input channels and resized to 128 × 128 128times 128 pixels. Each channel is independently min-max normalized to [ 0 , 1 ] [0,1] . \n127\t\n128\t Temporal Segmentation \n129\t\n130\t As illustrated in Fig.  2 , we use different temporal segmentation strategies during training and testing. During training, each audio recording is randomly cropped to a single 400 ms segment, which serves as an independent training sample. Recordings shorter than 400 ms are zero-padded: during training, the audio is placed at a random position within the target window; during validation and test, it is center-aligned. This random cropping acts as a form of data augmentation, exposing the model to different temporal regions of the same vocalization across training epochs and improving robustness to temporal variability. \n131\t\n132\t The choice of 400 ms segments provides enough temporal context for both datasets and is motivated by bioacoustic considerations: Individual pig vocalizations have typical durations ranging from 0.3 to 1.8 seconds (Briefer et al. , 2022 ) . Hence, a 400 ms window likely captures the most discriminative part of a call without padding artifacts for the majority of samples. For DogBark, individual bark events are also short (typically well under one second), and the 400 ms window likely captures a complete bark unit. Shorter segments risk capturing only partial vocal units, reducing temporal context, while segments longer than a single call risk mixing adjacent events or silent gaps. \n133\t\n134\t Data Augmentation \n135\t\n136\t To improve model robustness to acoustic variability in realistic farm environments and prevent overfitting, we apply additive Gaussian noise during training. For each training sample, noise is generated from a standard normal distribution and scaled to achieve a signal-to-noise ratio (SNR) uniformly sampled from the range [-5, 15] dB. \n137\t\n138\t 4.3 Training Procedure \n139\t\n140\t The training process consists of two stages: hyperparameter optimization followed by a final training. \n141\t\n142\t Stage 1: Hyperparameter Optimization \n143\t\n144\t We use Bayesian optimization implemented via the Python framework Optuna (Akiba et al. , 2019 ) with a Tree-structured Parzen Estimator (TPE) sampler and a median pruner to run 50 trials. Each trial trains and evaluates the model using the previously described training and validation data. To prevent further class imbalance between folds for the SoundWel dataset, we did not employ cross-validation. For DogBark, we adopted the same strategy for consistency. Training runs for up to 300 epochs with early stopping (patience 20, monitored on validation macro-averaged F1-scores). The trial that yields the highest score is selected for final training. The hyperparameter search space covers: learning rate ∈ [ 10 − 5 , 10 − 3 ] in[10^{-5},10^{-3}] , latent dimension D ∈ { 64 , 128 , 256 } Din{64,128,256} , softmax temperature τ ∈ [ 0.5 , 2.0 ] tauin[0.5,2.0] , batch size ∈ { 16 , 32 , 64 } in{16,32,64} , weight decay ∈ [ 10 − 6 , 10 − 4 ] in[10^{-6},10^{-4}] , classification loss weight λ cls ∈ [ 0.5 , 2.0 ] lambda_{text{cls}}in[0.5,2.0] , intra-modal DC loss weights λ DC_a , λ DC_f ∈ [ 2 × 10 − 3 , 2 × 10 − 1 ] lambda_{text{DC_a}},lambda_{text{DC_f}}in[2{times}10^{-3},2{times}10^{-1}] , cross-modal DC loss weight λ DC_af ∈ [ 2 × 10 − 3 , 2 × 10 − 1 ] lambda_{text{DC_af}}in[2{times}10^{-3},2{times}10^{-1}] , and variance regularization weight λ regu ∈ [ 2 × 10 − 5 , 2 × 10 − 3 ] lambda_{text{regu}}in[2{times}10^{-5},2{times}10^{-3}] . \n145\t\n146\t Stage 2: Final Model Training \n147\t\n148\t Using the best hyperparameter configuration from Stage 1, the training and validation data are merged, such that the final model trains on all non-test data. No early stopping is applied; instead, we scale the number of training epochs based on the results of the hyperparameter optimization (HPO): \n149\t\n150\t T final = round ⁡ ( T hpo × N hpo N final ) , T_{text{final}}=operatorname{round}!left(T_{text{hpo}}timesfrac{N_{text{hpo}}}{N_{text{final}}}right), \n151\t\n152\t (16) \n153\t\n154\t where T hpo T_{text{hpo}} is the epoch at which early stopping was conducted for the best HPO trial, N hpo N_{text{hpo}} is the training-set size during HPO, and N final N_{text{final}} is the size of the combined training and validation data. For the final training, we use the AdamW optimizer (Loshchilov and Hutter, 2019 ) with a linear warm-up schedule (learning rate rises from lr × 10 − 3 mathrm{lr}times 10^{-3} to lr mathrm{lr} over 10 epochs) followed by cosine annealing to a minimum of 10 − 6 10^{-6} . \n155\t\n156\t The identity-constrained test partitions are rather small (160 recordings for DogBark, 758 for SoundWel). To yield a reliable performance assessment and reduce the effect of randomness in training, we repeat Stage 2 five times and provide an averaged performance over five seeds. The performance variation is, for instance, caused by random weight initialization, per-step reparameterization noise ( z = μ z + σ z ⊙ ε , ε ∼ 𝒩 ​ ( 0 , I ) z=mu_{z}+sigma_{z}odotvarepsilon, varepsilonsimmathcal{N}(0,I) ), and stochastic data augmentation. \n157\t\n158\t 4.4 Test-Time Inference and Segment Aggregation \n159\t\n160\t At test time, audio recordings are processed at their full length rather than as single random crops, ensuring that all temporal information is utilized for classification. As shown in Fig.  2 , each recording is partitioned into overlapping segments of 400 ms duration with 50% overlap, producing several chunks. Recordings shorter than 400 ms are represented by a single chunk and right-padded with zeros to the target length. \n161\t\n162\t Each chunk is independently processed by the model to produce a per-chunk prediction, and the final recording-level prediction is determined by a majority vote across all chunks. We used this strategy since the overlapping chunks ensure comprehensive coverage of the recording, and the majority vote is robust to individual chunks containing noise or ambiguous features. \n163\t\n164\t 4.5 Implementation Details \n165\t\n166\t All experiments were implemented with PyTorch Lightning and trained on NVIDIA RTX 6000, with 48GB VRAM using Distributed Data Parallelism and 16-bit mixed-precision arithmetic. All hyperparameters are HPO-selected independently per model variant and dataset; exact values are recorded in the experiment configuration files released with the code. Both the waveform and spectrogram encoders are trained from random initialization; no pretrained weights are used. All variants share identical backbone architectures and training conditions to ensure that performance differences reflect the fusion strategy rather than encoder capacity. The Concatenation baseline uses the same dual ResNet-18 encoders and mean-pooling aggregation as the proposed UAF model; the two 512-dimensional utterance embeddings are concatenated into a 1,024-dimensional vector and passed to a linear classification head trained with cross-entropy only. The waveform-only and spectrogram-only baselines likewise use single-branch cross-entropy. The UAF uncertainty heads ( W μ ( m ) W_{mu}^{(m)} and W σ ( m ) W_{sigma}^{(m)} in Eq.  3 ) add approximately 262K model parameters over the Concatenation baseline when D z = 128 D_{z}=128 . All code is publicly available on GitHub at https://github.com/smAIL-WS/Uncertainty-Aware-Crossmodal-Fusion-for-Classification-of-Animal-Behavior \n167\t\n168\t Figure 2: Training and testing procedures. Left: during training, random 400 ms crops are extracted from each recording. Right: during testing, overlapping 400 ms sliding windows with 50% overlap are applied, and predictions are aggregated across all windows. \n169\t\n170\t 5 Results and Discussion \n171\t\n172\t We first provide a quantitative performance comparison of our proposed UAF framework on both benchmark datasets. Then, we further analyze the per-class results, the prediction calibration and confidence, as well as the fusion weight adaptation if one of the representations is corrupted or missing. Model performance is evaluated on held-out test data, with all metrics reported as mean and standard deviation over five independent training runs. \n173\t\n174\t 5.1 Quantitative Performance Comparison \n175\t\n176\t In Table  1 , we compare seven model variants: two unimodal baselines ((i) waveform-only, (ii) spectrogram-only), (iii) simple concatenation fusion, and four uncertainty-aware fusion (UAF) configurations differing in their temporal module ((iv) mean pooling, (v) GRU, (vi) Transformer, (vii) attention pooling). \n177\t\n178\t On the SoundWel dataset, the spectrogram-only baseline (57.7% acc., 37.2% F1) substantially outperforms the waveform-only baseline (52.2% acc., 34.7% F1), confirming that frequency-domain representations capture more discriminative information for the pig vocalization context. Further, simple concatenation (56.4% acc., 34.3% F1) underperforms the spectrogram-only on both metrics. Hence, merging a weaker waveform stream with a stronger spectrogram without accounting for their relative reliability can dilute the discriminative frequency features. All UAF variants avoid this degradation, which means the uncertainty weighting shifts contribution toward whichever modality produces more confident embeddings. UAF (mean pooling) achieves the best macro-averaged F1-score (39.7%  ± pm  1.3%), while UAF (attention pooling) achieves the highest accuracy (60.3%  ± pm  2.5%). Our experimental results thus indicate that the primary performance advantage on SoundWel is the avoidance of concatenation degradation ( − - 2.9 F1 vs. spectrogram-only) rather than a large absolute gain over the best unimodal baseline. The GRU and Transformer variants are competitive but show larger run-to-run variance, which we attribute to the larger hypothesis space of these models on a dataset of limited size. \n179\t\n180\t On the DogBark dataset, UAF (mean pooling) is the best-performing model across both metrics (73.1%  ± pm  6.9% acc., 71.5%  ± pm  5.7% F1), outperforming spectrogram-only by 4.0 percent in accuracy and 8.3 percent in F1-score, and outperforming concatenation by 10.7 and 12.1 percent, respectively. This large margin over concatenation indicates that the adaptive modality weighting of UAF provides a meaningful robustness benefit for small datasets such as DogBark. UAF (Transformer) exhibits unstable training (63.3%  ± pm  11.0% acc., 51.9%  ± pm  22.4% F1), with some runs converging well and others collapsing, a known limitation of Transformers when training data per class is limited. Further, UAF (attention pooling) (57.0%  ± pm  6.2% acc.) underperforms the concatenation method on DogBark, suggesting that the attention pooling aggregation may overfit for DogBark with only six training identities. \n181\t\n182\t Model \n183\t\n184\t SoundWel (pig-identity split, 13/17 classes in test) \n185\t\n186\t DogBark (3 classes, dog-identity split) \n187\t\n188\t Acc. (%) \n189\t Macro F1 (%) \n190\t Acc. (%) \n191\t Macro F1 (%) \n192\t\n193\t Waveform-only \n194\t 52.2 ± 2.0 52.2pm 2.0 \n195\t 34.7 ± 1.2 34.7pm 1.2 \n196\t 53.6 ± 2.2 53.6pm 2.2 \n197\t 44.0 ± 11.2 44.0pm 11.2 \n198\t\n199\t Spectrogram-only \n200\t 57.7 ± 2.1 57.7pm 2.1 \n201\t 37.2 ± 3.1 37.2pm 3.1 \n202\t 69.1 ± 3.9 69.1pm 3.9 \n203\t 63.2 ± 5.1 63.2pm 5.1 \n204\t\n205\t Concatenation \n206\t 56.4 ± 2.4 56.4pm 2.4 \n207\t 34.3 ± 3.7 34.3pm 3.7 \n208\t 62.4 ± 5.2 62.4pm 5.2 \n209\t 59.4 ± 5.3 59.4pm 5.3 \n210\t\n211\t UAF (mean pooling) \n212\t 59.4 ± 1.9 59.4pm 1.9 \n213\t 39.7 ± 1.3 mathbf{39.7pm 1.3} \n214\t 73.1 ± 6.9 mathbf{73.1pm 6.9} \n215\t 71.5 ± 5.7 mathbf{71.5pm 5.7} \n216\t\n217\t UAF (GRU) \n218\t 58.8 ± 3.7 58.8pm 3.7 \n219\t 37.8 ± 4.2 37.8pm 4.2 \n220\t 68.1 ± 3.5 68.1pm 3.5 \n221\t 61.0 ± 6.2 61.0pm 6.2 \n222\t\n223\t UAF (Transformer) \n224\t 57.0 ± 3.0 57.0pm 3.0 \n225\t 38.4 ± 4.0 38.4pm 4.0 \n226\t 63.3 ± 11.0 63.3pm 11.0 \n227\t 51.9 ± 22.4 51.9pm 22.4 \n228\t\n229\t UAF (Att. Pooling) \n230\t 60.3 ± 2.5 mathbf{60.3pm 2.5} \n231\t 38.0 ± 1.3 38.0pm 1.3 \n232\t 57.0 ± 6.2 57.0pm 6.2 \n233\t 43.0 ± 10.4 43.0pm 10.4 \n234\t\n235\t Table 1: Classification performance under identity-based splits. In SoundWel the pig identity inferred from filename, recording year, and recording team). Macro-F1 is computed over the 13 of 17 classes present in the test partition. In DogBark, dogs named Mac and Zoe are considred as the test set. Metrics are mean  ± pm  std over 5 independent training runs. Bold shows the best performing model. \n236\t\n237\t Role of temporal architecture \n238\t\n239\t Leveraging a GRU or Transformer component within our UAF framework does not yield consistent performance gains in comparison with a simple mean pooling (Table  1 ), and increases run-to-run variance. This indicates that local chunk-level uncertainty estimation is sufficient and that introducing sequence dependencies may cause overfitting rather than generalization. We therefore recommend mean pooling as the default temporal module for uncertainty-aware bioacoustic fusion at current dataset scales and focus our results analysis on this variant in the following sections. \n240\t\n241\t 5.2 Per-Class Classification Analysis \n242\t\n243\t Figure  3 shows normalized confusion matrices for the UAF (mean pooling) model on both datasets. On DogBark (Fig.  3 , left), although there is an imbalance in the number of samples across behavioral classes, recall remains balanced (aggression 0.64, contact 0.80, play 0.75). The largest confusions occur between aggression and contact barks, both of which are broadband, high-energy temporal calls. For SoundWel (Fig.  3 , right), we show five classes that represent both acute and chronic welfare challenges (see Fig.  1 for the complete 17-class matrix). Reunion is classified near-perfectly. Restrain in contrast is the most challenging class, frequently confused with castration and isolation , which is consistent with the overlapping high-stress acoustic profiles of procedural contexts. The contrast between barren (well-recognized) and restrain (poorly recognized) is noteworthy: barren -housing grunts are tonally distinct, whereas restrain calls overlap spectrally with other acute-stress vocalizations. This suggests that the primary remaining challenge on SoundWel lies in distinguishing between welfare-negative calls. \n244\t\n245\t Figure 3: Normalized confusion matrices for UAF (mean pooling) on DogBark (left, 3 classes) and SoundWel (right, 5 welfare-relevant classes). See the full 17-class SoundWel matrix in A , Fig.  1 . \n246\t\n247\t 5.3 Prediction Calibration \n248\t\n249\t Beyond improving classification accuracy, the uncertainty-aware design aims to produce confidence estimates that are well calibrated. Hence, a prediction reported with, e.g., 90% confidence should be correct roughly 90% of the time. We assess this along several complementary metrics. Figure  4 and   5 visualize this assessment, while Table  2 quantifies it numerically. The Expected Calibration Error (ECE) measures the average gap between stated confidence and actual accuracy, and ECE cal {}_{text{cal}} reports this same gap after a simple post-hoc temperature-scaling fix (Guo et al. , 2017 ) , showing how much of the miscalibration is easily correctable. The AUROC of the latent uncertainty norm ‖ σ z ‖ |sigma_{z}| (Efron, 1979 ) tests whether the model’s own uncertainty signal can distinguish between correct and incorrect predictions, while the Mann–Whitney U test (Mann and Whitney, 1947 ) checks whether σ z sigma_{z} is statistically significantly larger for incorrect predictions than for correct ones. The Brier score (Brier, 1950 ) folds accuracy and calibration into a single proper scoring rule, penalizing confident mistakes most heavily. \n250\t\n251\t Figure 4: Reliability diagrams for UAF (mean pooling) on DogBark (left) and SoundWel (right). A perfectly calibrated model lies on the dashed diagonal. Points above indicate under-confidence (the model is more accurate than its stated confidence), and points below indicate over-confidence. \n252\t\n253\t The reliability diagrams in Fig.  4 shows that our model is modest about its accuracy for DogBark, while it is overconfident for Soundwel. The reason could be that the DogBark dataset has long calls of the dogs and majority voting over many sliding-window chunks helps to recover the correct label more often than any individual chunk’s own confidence would suggest. On the other hand, Soundwel reflects its greater dataset complexity. However, in Table  2 , ECE evaluations show that the reliablity for Soundwel could be corrected at test phase by fitting a scalar temperature. Further, the Brier scores (0.199 for DogBark, 0.217 for SoundWel) indicate the model’s performance based on accuracy and uncertainty is well below the worst-case value of 1. \n254\t\n255\t The Difficulty-Confidence loss (see Section 3.7 ) pushes harder samples toward lower values of σ z sigma_{z} . The AUROC (0.415 for DogBark, 0.337 for SoundWel) and p MWU p_{text{MWU}} (0.943 for DogBark, 1.000 for SoundWel) values in Table  2 confirm this statement. Consequently, the latent uncertainty could be used as a misclassification signal, such that a low σ z sigma_{z} indicates that the recording is most likely misclassified. For example, see Figure 5 , where the distribution of correctly classified samples for the SoundWel dataset has a larger mean. However, this is not a strong misclassification signal for the DogBark dataset, which we attribute to the limited size of this dataset, with only 122 correctly classified and 38 misclassified samples. \n256\t\n257\t Figure 5: Distribution of ‖ σ z ‖ |sigma_{z}| (latent uncertainty norm) for UAF (mean pooling) on DogBark (left) and SoundWel (right). Green: correctly classified recordings; red: incorrectly classified. \n258\t\n259\t Table 2: Uncertainty cal\n260\t\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "ac020b21-6746-4c7c-b94a-3a5b96c32d4d", "uuid": "c4d78bd1-d5fe-48e6-b223-4f2c70a14fa5", "timestamp": "2026-08-04T07:34:45.096Z", "tool_use_result": {"type": "text", "file": {"filePath": "/inspire/qb-ilm/project/video-generation/chenxie-25019/hyr/iHateTheWorld555.github.io/.claude-review/projects/-inspire-qb-ilm-project-video-generation-chenxie-25019-hyr-iHateTheWorld555-github-io/ac020b21-6746-4c7c-b94a-3a5b96c32d4d/tool-results/bcubjuaaa.txt", "content": "er the dimensionality of 𝝁 m boldsymbol{mu}_{m} and 𝝈 m boldsymbol{sigma}_{m} as a hyperparameter ( D z D_{z} ) and optimize it using Bayesian optimization leveraging the Python framework Optuna (Akiba et al. , 2019 ) (see Section 4.3 ). \n\n 3.6 Dimension-wise Uncertainty Fusion \n\n Given the Gaussian parameters ( 𝝁 A , 𝝈 A ) (boldsymbol{mu}_{A},boldsymbol{sigma}_{A}) and ( 𝝁 F , 𝝈 F ) (boldsymbol{mu}_{F},boldsymbol{sigma}_{F}) , fusion weights are computed independently for each latent dimension d ∈ { 1 , … , D z } din{1,ldots,D_{z}} : \n\n w A ​ ( d ) = σ F ​ ( d ) σ A ​ ( d ) + σ F ​ ( d ) , w F ​ ( d ) = σ A ​ ( d ) σ A ​ ( d ) + σ F ​ ( d ) . w_{A}(d)=frac{sigma_{F}(d)}{sigma_{A}(d)+sigma_{F}(d)},qquad w_{F}(d)=frac{sigma_{A}(d)}{sigma_{A}(d)+sigma_{F}(d)}. \n\n (4) \n\n Consequently, a dimension in which the spectrogram is highly uncertain ( σ F ​ ( d ) ≫ σ A ​ ( d ) sigma_{F}(d)ggsigma_{A}(d) ) receives proportionally more weight from the waveform, and vice versa. Stacking these per-dimension weights into vectors 𝐰 A , 𝐰 F ∈ ℝ D z mathbf{w}_{A},mathbf{w}_{F}inmathbb{R}^{D_{z}} (with 𝐰 A + 𝐰 F = 𝟏 mathbf{w}_{A}+mathbf{w}_{F}=mathbf{1} ), the fused Gaussian parameters are defined as: \n\n 𝝁 z displaystyleboldsymbol{mu}_{z} \n = 𝐰 A ⊙ 𝝁 A + 𝐰 F ⊙ 𝝁 F , displaystyle=mathbf{w}_{A}odotboldsymbol{mu}_{A}+mathbf{w}_{F}odotboldsymbol{mu}_{F}, \n\n (5) \n\n 𝝈 z displaystyleboldsymbol{sigma}_{z} \n = 𝐰 A ⊙ 𝝈 A + 𝐰 F ⊙ 𝝈 F . displaystyle=mathbf{w}_{A}odotboldsymbol{sigma}_{A}+mathbf{w}_{F}odotboldsymbol{sigma}_{F}. \n\n (6) \n\n During training, a latent sample is drawn via the reparameterization trick: \n\n 𝐳 = 𝝁 z + 𝝈 z ⊙ 𝜺 , 𝜺 ∼ 𝒩 ​ ( 𝟎 , 𝐈 ) . mathbf{z}=boldsymbol{mu}_{z}+boldsymbol{sigma}_{z}odotboldsymbol{varepsilon},quadboldsymbol{varepsilon}simmathcal{N}(mathbf{0},mathbf{I}). \n\n (7) \n\n At inference, we set 𝐳 = 𝝁 z mathbf{z}=boldsymbol{mu}_{z} . The vector 𝐳 mathbf{z} is then further processed by a dropout-regularized linear layer to produce class logits ℓ ^ z = W c ​ 𝐳 ∈ ℝ K hat{ell}_{z}=W_{c}mathbf{z}inmathbb{R}^{K} . \n\n 3.7 Training Objective \n\n Inspired by Tellamekala et al. ( 2023 ) and Xie et al. ( 2024b ) , we design a novel loss function that supervises classification from the fused and unimodal predictions, and simultaneously learns the uncertainty to help a dynamic fusion based on the confidence of the modalities. In the following, we explain each component of this loss function. \n\n Classification loss \n\n Each unimodal stream shown in Fig.  1 has an independent linear head on top of 𝝁 A boldsymbol{mu}_{A} and 𝝁 F boldsymbol{mu}_{F} , allowing it to learn the class label directly from that modality. The fused branch (logits from 𝐳 mathbf{z} ) is additionally supervised using the same class label. Together, the three branches form the classification loss: \n\n ℒ cls = 1 3 ​ [ ℋ ​ ( ℓ ^ z , y ) + ℋ ​ ( ℓ ^ A , y ) + ℋ ​ ( ℓ ^ F , y ) ] , mathcal{L}_{mathrm{cls}}=frac{1}{3}Bigl[mathcal{H}(hat{ell}_{z},,y)+mathcal{H}(hat{ell}_{A},,y)+mathcal{H}(hat{ell}_{F},,y)Bigr], \n\n (8) \n\n where ℋ ​ ( ⋅ , y ) mathcal{H}(cdot,y) denotes cross-entropy with the ground-truth label y y , and ℓ ^ m = W m ​ 𝝁 m hat{ell}_{m}=W_{m}boldsymbol{mu}_{m} . During training, ℓ ^ z hat{ell}_{z} is computed from the reparameterized sample, see Eq.  7 , while in the inference phase, the unimodal classifiers receive means 𝝁 A boldsymbol{mu}_{A} and 𝝁 F boldsymbol{mu}_{F} without the reparameterization noise. \n\n Difficulty-Confidence loss \n\n The fusion process requires uncertainty scores. However, no ground-truth labels for uncertainty are available. Therefore, we compare within each mini-batch of B B samples how hard a sample actually was to classify against how confident the model was about its prediction. This allows the model to learn meaningful uncertainty estimates from relative comparisons within a batch. \n\n For each modality m ∈ { A , F } min{A,F} and sample i i , we compute a difficulty score (the sample’s own cross-entropy loss) and a confidence score (the inverse of its predicted uncertainty): \n\n d m ( i ) = ℋ ​ ( ℓ ^ m ( i ) , y ( i ) ) , s m ( i ) = 1 ‖ 𝝈 m ( i ) ‖ 2 + ε . d_{m}^{(i)}=mathcal{H}(hat{ell}_{m}^{(i)},,y^{(i)}),qquad s_{m}^{(i)}=frac{1}{|boldsymbol{sigma}_{m}^{(i)}|_{2}+varepsilon}. \n\n (9) \n\n Both scores are then normalized via a softmax with temperature τ tau over all B B samples into two probability distributions describing how difficult and how confident each sample is relative to the rest of the batch : \n\n 𝐩 D m = softmax ​ ( 𝐝 m / τ ) , 𝐩 S m = softmax ​ ( 𝐬 m / τ ) . mathbf{p}_{D}^{m}=mathrm{softmax}!left(mathbf{d}_{m},/,tauright),qquadmathbf{p}_{S}^{m}=mathrm{softmax}!left(mathbf{s}_{m},/,tauright). \n\n (10) \n\n Matching these two distributions via a symmetric KL divergence encourages samples that rank as relatively difficult under 𝐩 D m mathbf{p}_{D}^{m} to also rank as relatively confident under 𝐩 S m mathbf{p}_{S}^{m} , which corresponds to lower 𝝈 m boldsymbol{sigma}_{m} . In contrast, easy samples are encouraged to have lower confidence (higher 𝝈 m boldsymbol{sigma}_{m} ). Thus, the loss couples relative difficulty to relative confidence. The results in Section 5.3 show the correlation between ‖ σ z ‖ |sigma_{z}| and the misclassification error. For each modality m m , this correlation is imposed with the symmetric KL divergence: \n\n ℒ DC , m = KL ​ ( 𝐩 D m ∥ 𝐩 S m ) + KL ​ ( 𝐩 S m ∥ 𝐩 D m ) , m ∈ { A , F } . mathcal{L}_{mathrm{DC},m}=mathrm{KL}!left(mathbf{p}_{D}^{m},|,mathbf{p}_{S}^{m}right)+mathrm{KL}!left(mathbf{p}_{S}^{m},|,mathbf{p}_{D}^{m}right),quad min{A,F}. \n\n (11) \n\n To apply the same idea jointly (as a single loss term) across both modalities, we interleave the modalities’ difficulty and confidence scores into 2 ​ B 2B -length vectors: \n\n 𝐝 A ​ F = [ d A ( 1 ) , d F ( 1 ) , … , d A ( B ) , d F ( B ) ] , 𝐬 A ​ F = [ s A ( 1 ) , s F ( 1 ) , … , s A ( B ) , s F ( B ) ] . mathbf{d}_{AF}=bigl[d_{A}^{(1)},,d_{F}^{(1)},,ldots,,d_{A}^{(B)},,d_{F}^{(B)}bigr],quadmathbf{s}_{AF}=bigl[s_{A}^{(1)},,s_{F}^{(1)},,ldots,,s_{A}^{(B)},,s_{F}^{(B)}bigr]. \n\n (12) \n\n The same symmetric KL penalty is then applied to this combined set of 2 ​ B 2B samples: \n\n ℒ DC , A ​ F = KL ​ ( 𝐩 D A ​ F ∥ 𝐩 S A ​ F ) + KL ​ ( 𝐩 S A ​ F ∥ 𝐩 D A ​ F ) . mathcal{L}_{mathrm{DC},AF}=mathrm{KL}!left(mathbf{p}_{D}^{AF},|,mathbf{p}_{S}^{AF}right)+mathrm{KL}!left(mathbf{p}_{S}^{AF},|,mathbf{p}_{D}^{AF}right). \n\n (13) \n\n Variance regularizer \n\n To prevent variance collapse (the model pushing 𝝈 m → 0 boldsymbol{sigma}_{m}to 0 to suppress noisy gradients), we penalize departure from an isotropic standard normal prior (Tellamekala et al. , 2023 ; Xie et al. , 2024b ) : \n\n ℒ R = ∑ m ∈ { A , F } KL ​ ( 𝒩 ​ ( 𝝁 m , 𝝈 m 2 ) ∥ 𝒩 ​ ( 𝟎 , 𝐈 ) ) = − 1 2 ​ ∑ m ∑ d = 1 D z [ 1 + log ⁡ σ m , d 2 − μ m , d 2 − σ m , d 2 ] , begin{split}mathcal{L}_{R}&=sum_{min{A,F}}mathrm{KL}!left(mathcal{N}(boldsymbol{mu}_{m},boldsymbol{sigma}_{m}^{2}),big|,mathcal{N}(mathbf{0},mathbf{I})right)\n&=-frac{1}{2}sum_{m}sum_{d=1}^{D_{z}}Bigl[1+logsigma_{m,d}^{2}-mu_{m,d}^{2}-sigma_{m,d}^{2}Bigr],end{split} \n\n (14) \n\n averaged over the batch. \n\n The total training objective composed of the above-described components is finally formulated as: \n\n ℒ = λ cls ​ ℒ cls + λ DC , A ​ ℒ DC , A + λ DC , F ​ ℒ DC , F + λ DC , A ​ F ​ ℒ DC , A ​ F + λ R ​ ℒ R , mathcal{L}=lambda_{mathrm{cls}},mathcal{L}_{mathrm{cls}}+lambda_{mathrm{DC},A},mathcal{L}_{mathrm{DC},A}+lambda_{mathrm{DC},F},mathcal{L}_{mathrm{DC},F}+lambda_{mathrm{DC},AF},mathcal{L}_{mathrm{DC},AF}+lambda_{R},mathcal{L}_{R}, \n\n (15) \n\n where default weights are set empirically to λ cls = 1 lambda_{mathrm{cls}}=1 , λ DC , A = λ DC , F = λ DC , A ​ F = 10 − 3 lambda_{mathrm{DC},A}=lambda_{mathrm{DC},F}=lambda_{mathrm{DC},AF}=10^{-3} , λ R = 10 − 4 lambda_{R}=10^{-4} . \n\n 4 Experimental Setup \n\n 4.1 Datasets and Data Splits \n\n We evaluate the proposed uncertainty-aware fusion framework on two publicly available datasets representing (i) different animal vocalization classification tasks to assess cross-species generalization and (ii) different task complexities: pig calls and dog barks context classification. \n\n SoundWel Dataset \n\n The SoundWel dataset (Briefer et al. , 2022 ) is a multi-institutional collection of pig vocalizations under the SoundWel research program. It contains 6,887 labeled audio clips recorded by six independent research teams across Europe (ETHZ, FBN, IASPA, IASPB, IASPC, NMBU), covering three age groups (piglet, weaner, growing/finishing) and 17 behavioral context categories spanning welfare-negative states (e.g. isolation, castration, crushing), welfare-positive states (e.g. enriched, reunion), and nursing-related behaviors. The dataset is severely class-imbalanced: For instance, isolation accounts for 29.7% of samples, while surprise accounts for only 0.2%. Individual clip durations have a mean of 0.36 s and a maximum of 3.6 s. \n\n DogBark Dataset \n\n The DogBark dataset (Yin and McCowan, 2004 ) contains dog vocalizations from ten individual dogs spanning six breeds, annotated across three behavioral contexts: aggression (14.3%), contact (55.6%), and play (30.2%). The original dataset comprises 4,672 samples; however, only 693 labeled recordings are publicly available. \n\n Data Splits \n\n A random stratified split would cause the recordings of the same individual to appear in both training and test sets, encouraging the model to exploit individual-specific vocal fingerprints rather than context-discriminative features. This is known as data leakage between train and test sets, which unrealistically inflates the model’s performance during the test phase (Bernett et al. , 2024 ) . Instead, in this work, we considered identity-aware splits for both datasets. However, results under a random stratified split are also provided in Table  1 ( B ) for comparison with the previously published result of Briefer et al. ( 2022 ) . \n\n The SoundWel dataset does not provide an identity train-val-test split, and only a limited number of samples have a pig ID. To avoid data leakage, we used the available metadata, i.e., filename patterns encoding per-animal or per-group identifiers (pen IDs, animal IDs, sow IDs, or litter numbers depending on team), recording year to disambiguate cohorts across experimental sessions, and recording team as a coarse grouping variable to prevent cross-team leakage. Recordings for which no parseable identity could be inferred (2,398 recordings, 34.8% of the dataset) are assigned to the training set such that the model could benefit from learning on more samples without the risk of data leakage. The resulting split is: train 5,452 (79.2%) | | val 677 (9.8%) | | test 758 (11.0%). All six recording teams contribute to every subset, and 13 of the 17 classes appear in the test set. The four absent classes in the test set (BeforeNursing, Fighting, Huddling, Run) have no recordings with parseable pig identities. Therefore, the macro-averaged F1-score is computed over the 13 classes present in the test partition. \n\n For the DogBark dataset, we ensure that individual dogs are assigned exclusively to one partition. The test set consists of Mac (German shorthaired pointer, male) and Zoe (Australian cattle dog, female), providing cross-breed and cross-sex generalization with all three behavioral classes represented. The validation set consists of Keri and Louie; the remaining six dogs form the training set. This data split leads to 458 train (66.1%), 75 val (10.8%), and 160 test (23.1%) samples. The class distribution is nearly identical across partitions, making the macro-averaged F1-score a fair evaluation metric. \n\n 4.2 Preprocessing and Data Augmentation \n\n Audio Preprocessing \n\n All audio recordings are resampled to 16 kHz sampling rate, which captures the relevant acoustic bandwidth for both pig and dog vocalizations (predominantly below 8 kHz) while reducing computational cost compared to higher sampling rates. This sampling rate aligns with standard practices in animal bioacoustics and enables stable training (Stowell, 2022 ) . \n\n For the waveform input ( x A x_{A} ), raw audio signals are peak-normalized by dividing by the absolute maximum amplitude, mapping each clip to a range of [ − 1 , 1 ] [-1,1] . For the spectrogram input ( x F x_{F} ), we compute a three-channel time-frequency representation using a Mel-filterbank applied to the power spectrogram (STFT with FFT size n fft = 1024 n_{text{fft}}=1024 , hop length = 256 =256 samples (16 ms), Hann window, and power = 2 =2 ). We extract 128 Mel-frequency bands spanning 0–8 kHz and convert to decibels (dB) to obtain the log-power Mel spectrogram. We then compute its first-order ( Δ Delta -Mel) and second-order temporal derivative ( Δ ​ Δ DeltaDelta -Mel). These three representations are stacked as input channels and resized to 128 × 128 128times 128 pixels. Each channel is independently min-max normalized to [ 0 , 1 ] [0,1] . \n\n Temporal Segmentation \n\n As illustrated in Fig.  2 , we use different temporal segmentation strategies during training and testing. During training, each audio recording is randomly cropped to a single 400 ms segment, which serves as an independent training sample. Recordings shorter than 400 ms are zero-padded: during training, the audio is placed at a random position within the target window; during validation and test, it is center-aligned. This random cropping acts as a form of data augmentation, exposing the model to different temporal regions of the same vocalization across training epochs and improving robustness to temporal variability. \n\n The choice of 400 ms segments provides enough temporal context for both datasets and is motivated by bioacoustic considerations: Individual pig vocalizations have typical durations ranging from 0.3 to 1.8 seconds (Briefer et al. , 2022 ) . Hence, a 400 ms window likely captures the most discriminative part of a call without padding artifacts for the majority of samples. For DogBark, individual bark events are also short (typically well under one second), and the 400 ms window likely captures a complete bark unit. Shorter segments risk capturing only partial vocal units, reducing temporal context, while segments longer than a single call risk mixing adjacent events or silent gaps. \n\n Data Augmentation \n\n To improve model robustness to acoustic variability in realistic farm environments and prevent overfitting, we apply additive Gaussian noise during training. For each training sample, noise is generated from a standard normal distribution and scaled to achieve a signal-to-noise ratio (SNR) uniformly sampled from the range [-5, 15] dB. \n\n 4.3 Training Procedure \n\n The training process consists of two stages: hyperparameter optimization followed by a final training. \n\n Stage 1: Hyperparameter Optimization \n\n We use Bayesian optimization implemented via the Python framework Optuna (Akiba et al. , 2019 ) with a Tree-structured Parzen Estimator (TPE) sampler and a median pruner to run 50 trials. Each trial trains and evaluates the model using the previously described training and validation data. To prevent further class imbalance between folds for the SoundWel dataset, we did not employ cross-validation. For DogBark, we adopted the same strategy for consistency. Training runs for up to 300 epochs with early stopping (patience 20, monitored on validation macro-averaged F1-scores). The trial that yields the highest score is selected for final training. The hyperparameter search space covers: learning rate ∈ [ 10 − 5 , 10 − 3 ] in[10^{-5},10^{-3}] , latent dimension D ∈ { 64 , 128 , 256 } Din{64,128,256} , softmax temperature τ ∈ [ 0.5 , 2.0 ] tauin[0.5,2.0] , batch size ∈ { 16 , 32 , 64 } in{16,32,64} , weight decay ∈ [ 10 − 6 , 10 − 4 ] in[10^{-6},10^{-4}] , classification loss weight λ cls ∈ [ 0.5 , 2.0 ] lambda_{text{cls}}in[0.5,2.0] , intra-modal DC loss weights λ DC_a , λ DC_f ∈ [ 2 × 10 − 3 , 2 × 10 − 1 ] lambda_{text{DC_a}},lambda_{text{DC_f}}in[2{times}10^{-3},2{times}10^{-1}] , cross-modal DC loss weight λ DC_af ∈ [ 2 × 10 − 3 , 2 × 10 − 1 ] lambda_{text{DC_af}}in[2{times}10^{-3},2{times}10^{-1}] , and variance regularization weight λ regu ∈ [ 2 × 10 − 5 , 2 × 10 − 3 ] lambda_{text{regu}}in[2{times}10^{-5},2{times}10^{-3}] . \n\n Stage 2: Final Model Training \n\n Using the best hyperparameter configuration from Stage 1, the training and validation data are merged, such that the final model trains on all non-test data. No early stopping is applied; instead, we scale the number of training epochs based on the results of the hyperparameter optimization (HPO): \n\n T final = round ⁡ ( T hpo × N hpo N final ) , T_{text{final}}=operatorname{round}!left(T_{text{hpo}}timesfrac{N_{text{hpo}}}{N_{text{final}}}right), \n\n (16) \n\n where T hpo T_{text{hpo}} is the epoch at which early stopping was conducted for the best HPO trial, N hpo N_{text{hpo}} is the training-set size during HPO, and N final N_{text{final}} is the size of the combined training and validation data. For the final training, we use the AdamW optimizer (Loshchilov and Hutter, 2019 ) with a linear warm-up schedule (learning rate rises from lr × 10 − 3 mathrm{lr}times 10^{-3} to lr mathrm{lr} over 10 epochs) followed by cosine annealing to a minimum of 10 − 6 10^{-6} . \n\n The identity-constrained test partitions are rather small (160 recordings for DogBark, 758 for SoundWel). To yield a reliable performance assessment and reduce the effect of randomness in training, we repeat Stage 2 five times and provide an averaged performance over five seeds. The performance variation is, for instance, caused by random weight initialization, per-step reparameterization noise ( z = μ z + σ z ⊙ ε , ε ∼ 𝒩 ​ ( 0 , I ) z=mu_{z}+sigma_{z}odotvarepsilon, varepsilonsimmathcal{N}(0,I) ), and stochastic data augmentation. \n\n 4.4 Test-Time Inference and Segment Aggregation \n\n At test time, audio recordings are processed at their full length rather than as single random crops, ensuring that all temporal information is utilized for classification. As shown in Fig.  2 , each recording is partitioned into overlapping segments of 400 ms duration with 50% overlap, producing several chunks. Recordings shorter than 400 ms are represented by a single chunk and right-padded with zeros to the target length. \n\n Each chunk is independently processed by the model to produce a per-chunk prediction, and the final recording-level prediction is determined by a majority vote across all chunks. We used this strategy since the overlapping chunks ensure comprehensive coverage of the recording, and the majority vote is robust to individual chunks containing noise or ambiguous features. \n\n 4.5 Implementation Details \n\n All experiments were implemented with PyTorch Lightning and trained on NVIDIA RTX 6000, with 48GB VRAM using Distributed Data Parallelism and 16-bit mixed-precision arithmetic. All hyperparameters are HPO-selected independently per model variant and dataset; exact values are recorded in the experiment configuration files released with the code. Both the waveform and spectrogram encoders are trained from random initialization; no pretrained weights are used. All variants share identical backbone architectures and training conditions to ensure that performance differences reflect the fusion strategy rather than encoder capacity. The Concatenation baseline uses the same dual ResNet-18 encoders and mean-pooling aggregation as the proposed UAF model; the two 512-dimensional utterance embeddings are concatenated into a 1,024-dimensional vector and passed to a linear classification head trained with cross-entropy only. The waveform-only and spectrogram-only baselines likewise use single-branch cross-entropy. The UAF uncertainty heads ( W μ ( m ) W_{mu}^{(m)} and W σ ( m ) W_{sigma}^{(m)} in Eq.  3 ) add approximately 262K model parameters over the Concatenation baseline when D z = 128 D_{z}=128 . All code is publicly available on GitHub at https://github.com/smAIL-WS/Uncertainty-Aware-Crossmodal-Fusion-for-Classification-of-Animal-Behavior \n\n Figure 2: Training and testing procedures. Left: during training, random 400 ms crops are extracted from each recording. Right: during testing, overlapping 400 ms sliding windows with 50% overlap are applied, and predictions are aggregated across all windows. \n\n 5 Results and Discussion \n\n We first provide a quantitative performance comparison of our proposed UAF framework on both benchmark datasets. Then, we further analyze the per-class results, the prediction calibration and confidence, as well as the fusion weight adaptation if one of the representations is corrupted or missing. Model performance is evaluated on held-out test data, with all metrics reported as mean and standard deviation over five independent training runs. \n\n 5.1 Quantitative Performance Comparison \n\n In Table  1 , we compare seven model variants: two unimodal baselines ((i) waveform-only, (ii) spectrogram-only), (iii) simple concatenation fusion, and four uncertainty-aware fusion (UAF) configurations differing in their temporal module ((iv) mean pooling, (v) GRU, (vi) Transformer, (vii) attention pooling). \n\n On the SoundWel dataset, the spectrogram-only baseline (57.7% acc., 37.2% F1) substantially outperforms the waveform-only baseline (52.2% acc., 34.7% F1), confirming that frequency-domain representations capture more discriminative information for the pig vocalization context. Further, simple concatenation (56.4% acc., 34.3% F1) underperforms the spectrogram-only on both metrics. Hence, merging a weaker waveform stream with a stronger spectrogram without accounting for their relative reliability can dilute the discriminative frequency features. All UAF variants avoid this degradation, which means the uncertainty weighting shifts contribution toward whichever modality produces more confident embeddings. UAF (mean pooling) achieves the best macro-averaged F1-score (39.7%  ± pm  1.3%), while UAF (attention pooling) achieves the highest accuracy (60.3%  ± pm  2.5%). Our experimental results thus indicate that the primary performance advantage on SoundWel is the avoidance of concatenation degradation ( − - 2.9 F1 vs. spectrogram-only) rather than a large absolute gain over the best unimodal baseline. The GRU and Transformer variants are competitive but show larger run-to-run variance, which we attribute to the larger hypothesis space of these models on a dataset of limited size. \n\n On the DogBark dataset, UAF (mean pooling) is the best-performing model across both metrics (73.1%  ± pm  6.9% acc., 71.5%  ± pm  5.7% F1), outperforming spectrogram-only by 4.0 percent in accuracy and 8.3 percent in F1-score, and outperforming concatenation by 10.7 and 12.1 percent, respectively. This large margin over concatenation indicates that the adaptive modality weighting of UAF provides a meaningful robustness benefit for small datasets such as DogBark. UAF (Transformer) exhibits unstable training (63.3%  ± pm  11.0% acc., 51.9%  ± pm  22.4% F1), with some runs converging well and others collapsing, a known limitation of Transformers when training data per class is limited. Further, UAF (attention pooling) (57.0%  ± pm  6.2% acc.) underperforms the concatenation method on DogBark, suggesting that the attention pooling aggregation may overfit for DogBark with only six training identities. \n\n Model \n\n SoundWel (pig-identity split, 13/17 classes in test) \n\n DogBark (3 classes, dog-identity split) \n\n Acc. (%) \n Macro F1 (%) \n Acc. (%) \n Macro F1 (%) \n\n Waveform-only \n 52.2 ± 2.0 52.2pm 2.0 \n 34.7 ± 1.2 34.7pm 1.2 \n 53.6 ± 2.2 53.6pm 2.2 \n 44.0 ± 11.2 44.0pm 11.2 \n\n Spectrogram-only \n 57.7 ± 2.1 57.7pm 2.1 \n 37.2 ± 3.1 37.2pm 3.1 \n 69.1 ± 3.9 69.1pm 3.9 \n 63.2 ± 5.1 63.2pm 5.1 \n\n Concatenation \n 56.4 ± 2.4 56.4pm 2.4 \n 34.3 ± 3.7 34.3pm 3.7 \n 62.4 ± 5.2 62.4pm 5.2 \n 59.4 ± 5.3 59.4pm 5.3 \n\n UAF (mean pooling) \n 59.4 ± 1.9 59.4pm 1.9 \n 39.7 ± 1.3 mathbf{39.7pm 1.3} \n 73.1 ± 6.9 mathbf{73.1pm 6.9} \n 71.5 ± 5.7 mathbf{71.5pm 5.7} \n\n UAF (GRU) \n 58.8 ± 3.7 58.8pm 3.7 \n 37.8 ± 4.2 37.8pm 4.2 \n 68.1 ± 3.5 68.1pm 3.5 \n 61.0 ± 6.2 61.0pm 6.2 \n\n UAF (Transformer) \n 57.0 ± 3.0 57.0pm 3.0 \n 38.4 ± 4.0 38.4pm 4.0 \n 63.3 ± 11.0 63.3pm 11.0 \n 51.9 ± 22.4 51.9pm 22.4 \n\n UAF (Att. Pooling) \n 60.3 ± 2.5 mathbf{60.3pm 2.5} \n 38.0 ± 1.3 38.0pm 1.3 \n 57.0 ± 6.2 57.0pm 6.2 \n 43.0 ± 10.4 43.0pm 10.4 \n\n Table 1: Classification performance under identity-based splits. In SoundWel the pig identity inferred from filename, recording year, and recording team). Macro-F1 is computed over the 13 of 17 classes present in the test partition. In DogBark, dogs named Mac and Zoe are considred as the test set. Metrics are mean  ± pm  std over 5 independent training runs. Bold shows the best performing model. \n\n Role of temporal architecture \n\n Leveraging a GRU or Transformer component within our UAF framework does not yield consistent performance gains in comparison with a simple mean pooling (Table  1 ), and increases run-to-run variance. This indicates that local chunk-level uncertainty estimation is sufficient and that introducing sequence dependencies may cause overfitting rather than generalization. We therefore recommend mean pooling as the default temporal module for uncertainty-aware bioacoustic fusion at current dataset scales and focus our results analysis on this variant in the following sections. \n\n 5.2 Per-Class Classification Analysis \n\n Figure  3 shows normalized confusion matrices for the UAF (mean pooling) model on both datasets. On DogBark (Fig.  3 , left), although there is an imbalance in the number of samples across behavioral classes, recall remains balanced (aggression 0.64, contact 0.80, play 0.75). The largest confusions occur between aggression and contact barks, both of which are broadband, high-energy temporal calls. For SoundWel (Fig.  3 , right), we show five classes that represent both acute and chronic welfare challenges (see Fig.  1 for the complete 17-class matrix). Reunion is classified near-perfectly. Restrain in contrast is the most challenging class, frequently confused with castration and isolation , which is consistent with the overlapping high-stress acoustic profiles of procedural contexts. The contrast between barren (well-recognized) and restrain (poorly recognized) is noteworthy: barren -housing grunts are tonally distinct, whereas restrain calls overlap spectrally with other acute-stress vocalizations. This suggests that the primary remaining challenge on SoundWel lies in distinguishing between welfare-negative calls. \n\n Figure 3: Normalized confusion matrices for UAF (mean pooling) on DogBark (left, 3 classes) and SoundWel (right, 5 welfare-relevant classes). See the full 17-class SoundWel matrix in A , Fig.  1 . \n\n 5.3 Prediction Calibration \n\n Beyond improving classification accuracy, the uncertainty-aware design aims to produce confidence estimates that are well calibrated. Hence, a prediction reported with, e.g., 90% confidence should be correct roughly 90% of the time. We assess this along several complementary metrics. Figure  4 and   5 visualize this assessment, while Table  2 quantifies it numerically. The Expected Calibration Error (ECE) measures the average gap between stated confidence and actual accuracy, and ECE cal {}_{text{cal}} reports this same gap after a simple post-hoc temperature-scaling fix (Guo et al. , 2017 ) , showing how much of the miscalibration is easily correctable. The AUROC of the latent uncertainty norm ‖ σ z ‖ |sigma_{z}| (Efron, 1979 ) tests whether the model’s own uncertainty signal can distinguish between correct and incorrect predictions, while the Mann–Whitney U test (Mann and Whitney, 1947 ) checks whether σ z sigma_{z} is statistically significantly larger for incorrect predictions than for correct ones. The Brier score (Brier, 1950 ) folds accuracy and calibration into a single proper scoring rule, penalizing confident mistakes most heavily. \n\n Figure 4: Reliability diagrams for UAF (mean pooling) on DogBark (left) and SoundWel (right). A perfectly calibrated model lies on the dashed diagonal. Points above indicate under-confidence (the model is more accurate than its stated confidence), and points below indicate over-confidence. \n\n The reliability diagrams in Fig.  4 shows that our model is modest about its accuracy for DogBark, while it is overconfident for Soundwel. The reason could be that the DogBark dataset has long calls of the dogs and majority voting over many sliding-window chunks helps to recover the correct label more often than any individual chunk’s own confidence would suggest. On the other hand, Soundwel reflects its greater dataset complexity. However, in Table  2 , ECE evaluations show that the reliablity for Soundwel could be corrected at test phase by fitting a scalar temperature. Further, the Brier scores (0.199 for DogBark, 0.217 for SoundWel) indicate the model’s performance based on accuracy and uncertainty is well below the worst-case value of 1. \n\n The Difficulty-Confidence loss (see Section 3.7 ) pushes harder samples toward lower values of σ z sigma_{z} . The AUROC (0.415 for DogBark, 0.337 for SoundWel) and p MWU p_{text{MWU}} (0.943 for DogBark, 1.000 for SoundWel) values in Table  2 confirm this statement. Consequently, the latent uncertainty could be used as a misclassification signal, such that a low σ z sigma_{z} indicates that the recording is most likely misclassified. For example, see Figure 5 , where the distribution of correctly classified samples for the SoundWel dataset has a larger mean. However, this is not a strong misclassification signal for the DogBark dataset, which we attribute to the limited size of this dataset, with only 122 correctly classified and 38 misclassified samples. \n\n Figure 5: Distribution of ‖ σ z ‖ |sigma_{z}| (latent uncertainty norm) for UAF (mean pooling) on DogBark (left) and SoundWel (right). Green: correctly classified recordings; red: incorrectly classified. \n\n Table 2: Uncertainty cal\n", "numLines": 260, "startLine": 1, "totalLines": 260}}}
{"type": "assistant", "message": {"id": "msg_1785828891035", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "tool_use", "id": "chatcmpl-tool-ab2026e80559504b", "name": "Bash", "input": {"command": "python3 -c \"\nimport re\nwith open('/tmp/uaf_html.html', 'r') as f:\n    html = f.read()\ntext = re.sub(r'<script[^>]*>.*?</script>', '', html, flags=re.DOTALL)\ntext = re.sub(r'<style[^>]*>.*?</style>', '', text, flags=re.DOTALL)\ntext = re.sub(r'<[^>]+>', ' ', text)\ntext = re.sub(r'&amp;', '&', text)\ntext = re.sub(r'&lt;', '<', text)\ntext = re.sub(r'&gt;', '>', text)\ntext = re.sub(r'&nbsp;', ' ', text)\ntext = re.sub(r'&#x27;', \\\"'\\\", text)\ntext = re.sub(r'\\\\\\\\', '', text)\ntext = re.sub(r'\\n\\s*\\n', '\\n\\n', text)\ntext = re.sub(r'  +', ' ', text)\nprint(text[60000:])\n\" 2>&1 | head -600", "description": "Extract remaining text from HTML"}}], "model": "GLM-5.2-w4a8", "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 0, "output_tokens": 0, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 0, "cache_read_input_tokens": 0}, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "ac020b21-6746-4c7c-b94a-3a5b96c32d4d", "uuid": "e63adaae-3203-4223-a43d-76d8163ee188"}
{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-ab2026e80559504b", "type": "tool_result", "content": "ibration metrics for UAF (mean pooling). \n\n Dataset \n ECE ↓ downarrow \n\n ECE ↓ cal {}_{text{cal}}downarrow \n\n AUROC \n Brier ↓ bin {}_{text{bin}}downarrow \n\n p MWU p_{text{MWU}} \n\n DogBark \n 0.195 \n 0.109 \n 0.415 [0.31,0.52] \n\n 0.199 \n 0.943 \n\n SoundWel \n 0.144 \n 0.074 \n 0.337 [0.30,0.38] \n\n 0.217 \n 1.000 \n\n 5.4 Prediction Confidence by Behavioral Context \n\n Figure  6 shows how confident correct predictions are within each behavioral class: a high median confidence with low spread indicates a class that the model recognizes consistently, while a low median confidence or wide spread indicates a class where the model is unsure between different possibilities, even if it ultimately predicts correctly. \n\n Figure 6: Per-class predicted confidence distributions for UAF (mean pooling) on DogBark (left, 3 classes) and SoundWel (right, 17 classes). Each violin shows the distribution of the model’s maximum softmax probability for correctly classified samples of that class. \n\n On DogBark, the model produces relatively higher-confidence predictions for aggression barks, which have a distinct broadband impulsive structure, and somewhat lower confidence for contact and play barks. This observation is consistent with the confusion matrix patterns. On SoundWel, confidence distributions are more varied across the 17 classes. These distributions are qualitatively informative for practitioners such that a class with a consistently low-confidence distribution is a candidate for targeted data collection or class-specific fine-tuning. \n\n 5.5 Fusion Weight Adaptation Under Targeted Modality Corruption \n\n As an additional robustness experiment, we trained a UAF-based model with Gaussian noise injected into one of the streams during training, including complete signal zeroing to simulate missing inputs and noisy barn environments. \n\n Figure 7: Adaptive modality fusion weights under controlled noise injection for a UAF model trained with deliberative noise augmentation. (a) Clean waveform and mel-spectrogram: fusion weights remain balanced when both siganls are clean and equally informative. (b) Clean spectrogram and noisy waveform at 0.6 s: waveform uncertainty rises, shifting fusion weight toward the spectrogram. (c) Clean waveform and noisy spectrogram at 0.6 s: fusion weight shifts toward the waveform. \n\n As shown in Fig.  7 , in the clean condition (panel a), fusion weights oscillate near 0.5 for both modalities throughout the recording. When Gaussian noise is injected into the waveform at 0.6 s (panel b), the variance weighting shifts to the clean spectrogram. The opposite behavior occurs when the spectrogram is corrupted (panel c) and waveform is the clean input of the model. This indicates that noise injection during training can improve the sensitivity of the fusion weight response in the test phase. \n\n 6 Conclusion \n\n We presented Uncertainty-Aware Fusion (UAF), a dual-stream architecture that estimates per-utterance Gaussian uncertainty for waveform and spectrogram representations and fuses them via dimension-wise uncertainty weighting. Under an evaluation on SoundWel and DogBark that excludes all individuals seen during training, UAF (mean pooling) consistently outperforms both single-modality baselines and static-concatenation fusion. A controlled temporal aggregation ablation shows that simple mean pooling mostly outperforms GRU, Transformer, and attention pooling at current dataset scales. This confirms that the performance improvement arises from uncertainty-driven fusion and not the temporal modeling depth. Calibration analysis of UAF (mean pooling) shows that uncertainty gating reliably improves classification accuracy. \n\n While our approach shows promising results, several directions remain for future research. Both datasets contain a limited number of individuals, resulting in a correspondingly small test pool. Therefore, multi-farm benchmarks with broad environmental variability are needed to characterize generalization at deployment scale. Furthermore, we currently focus on two distinct representations of acoustic monitoring, while the proposed method can be extended to additional sensing modalities. Future methodological work could involve collecting a multimodal dataset and extending UAF to multi-farm settings with complementary modalities, such as video or accelerometry, to address limitations in both scalability and multimodal integration. \n\n Declaration of competing interest \n\n The authors declare that they have no known competing financial interests or personal relationships that could have appeared to influence the work reported in this paper. \n\n CRediT authorship contribution statement \n\n Ehsan Yaghoubi: Conceptualization, Methodology, Software, Validation, Formal analysis, Investigation, Resources, Data curation, Writing – original draft, Visualization. Florian Haselbeck: Conceptualization, Supervision, Writing – review & editing, Funding acquisition. \n\n Data availability \n\n Both datasets used in this study are publicly accessible. The full-size unlabeled DogBark dataset (Yin and McCowan, 2004 ) is archived at https://archive.org/details/dog-barks-raw . We use the annotated version containing 693 samples, which is available at https://huggingface.co/datasets/cgeorgiaw/animal-sounds . The SoundWel dataset (Briefer et al. , 2022 ) is provided as a labeled audio database on Zenodo at https://zenodo.org/records/8252482 . All resources were accessed on 27 July 2026. \n\n Declaration of generative AI and AI-assisted technologies in the manuscript preparation process \n\n The authors used Google Scholar Labs, Claude Code, ChatGPT, Monica, Grok, Perplexity, and Gemini for improving the clarity of language, brainstorming, literature and dataset search, as well as a critical review of the manuscript. The authors subsequently reviewed and significantly revised all AI-assisted suggestions. The authors assume full responsibility for the content presented in this manuscript. \n\n Acknowledgments \n\n We gratefully acknowledge the support of the Free State of Bavaria for funding the positions of EY and FH via the Bavarian Hightech Agenda. The funder had no role in the study design, data collection and analysis, decision to publish, or preparation of the manuscript. \n\n We would like to thank Harshavardan Subramanian and Dr. Georg Back for their constructive discussions and valuable feedback during the development of this work. \n\n References \n\n T. Akiba, S. Sano, T. Yanase, T. Ohta, and M. Koyama (2019) \n Optuna: a next-generation hyperparameter optimization framework .\n\n In Proceedings of the 25th ACM SIGKDD International Conference on Knowledge Discovery and Data Mining ,\n\n pp. 2623–2631 .\n\n Cited by: §3.5 ,\n §4.3 .\n\n A. Baevski, Y. Zhou, A. Mohamed, and M. Auli (2020) \n Wav2vec 2.0: a framework for self-supervised learning of speech representations .\n\n In Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems ,\n\n Vol. 33 , pp. 12449–12460 .\n\n Cited by: §1 ,\n §2.3 .\n\n T. Baltrušaitis, C. Ahuja, and L. Morency (2019) \n Multimodal machine learning: a survey and taxonomy .\n\n IEEE Transactions on Pattern Analysis and Machine Intelligence 41 ( 2 ), pp. 423–443 .\n\n External Links: Document \n\n Cited by: §2.4 ,\n §2.4 .\n\n D. Berckmans (2017) \n General introduction to precision livestock farming .\n\n Animal Frontiers 7 ( 1 ), pp. 6–11 .\n\n External Links: Document \n\n Cited by: §1 .\n\n C. Bergler, S. Q. Smeele, S. A. Tyndel, A. Barnhill, S. T. Ortiz, A. K. Kalan, R. X. Cheng, S. Brinkløv, A. N. Osiecka, J. Tougaard, et al. (2022) \n ANIMAL-spot enables animal-independent signal detection and classification using deep learning .\n\n Scientific Reports 12 ( 1 ), pp. 21966 .\n\n Cited by: §2.2 ,\n §2.2 .\n\n J. Bernett, D. B. Blumenthal, 